tasks/BACKLOG.md:161:🚪 **THE SECURE WINDOW ANSWERS, AND ITS CLOCK RUNS (implementer 2026-09-06, `fix/secure-choice-refusal-both-cures`, scratch worktree `wt-secure`, port 5306; owner ruling verbatim: "(5) both") — F-MCAP-1 CURED ON BOTH SIDES.** REPRODUCED FIRST, on the pre-cure tree (`artifacts/secure-choice-refusal/probe.mjs`, the proven Claim policy on `the-claim` / `e1-the-claim-01`, submitting `[SECURE_CHOICE, ...plan]` every turn): **8,783 secure-window turns in 35 s, every view BYTE-IDENTICAL, `expiresInMs` pinned at 20,000, no termination** (killed at a 45 s cap; the air-wall prover burned 14,467, F-MCAP-2's draft 18 min at 110 % CPU) — `before-until-end.json`; the 50-turn sample `before.json` shows the same freeze with `refusalVisibleInOrders: false`. THE REAL MECHANISM, read rather than inherited: the refusal DID reach stderr (8,783 `gr-sim rejected orders:` lines), but `gr-sim.readOrders` re-reads stdin **without advancing the sim** until a submission is accepted, so a view-only rider saw the same view forever and the pending clock never moved. **CURE A (visible):** the refusal is published on `now.orders[]` as a `failed` record, id `refused-secure-window`, naming the first order that is NOT the choice, reason `SECURE_CHOICE_ONLY: while the secure window is open, the only accepted submission is a single SECURE_CHOICE; answer the choice, then re-send the rest of the plan.` — one exported constant (`SECURE_WINDOW_REFUSAL{,_ID,_MESSAGE}`) quoted by the door, the transport and the guard; it is a RECEIPT held outside `this.records`, so the plan the rider already had standing survives, it is never ticked, and it retires on the next accepted submission. `needsRider` flips true. The transport's own line carries the same words (`gr-sim.mjs` `rejectOrders`, documented as load-bearing; stdout stays strict NDJSON by design, so nothing bare is written there). **CURE B (the clock):** each REFUSED submission spends one fixed step (1/30 s) of the window in `HeadlessContractSim.submitOrders`; `secureWindowOpen` (pending AND time left) splits from `pendingSecure` and gates only the "exactly one SECURE_CHOICE" rule, so a spent window lets the next submission through and `step()` lands the default through the SAME expiry path. MEASURED AFTER: **601 secure-window turns (600 refusals + 1), `expiresInMs` 20,000 → 0 strictly decreasing, `defaultedSecure` 1, exit 0, 11 s** (`after-until-end.json`); the 50-turn A/B moves the clock 20,000 → 18,333 where it was frozen (`after.json`), same secured/waves/gold/kills/calls, only `eventLogHash` differs (a run WITH refusals; `calls` already distinguished them). UNTOUCHED, PROVEN: `null-floor-anchors --check` reports ONLY the pre-existing stale eraStamp (183.6 s, no floor moved); `artifacts/mare-claim-air-prevalent/prover-01.tape.json` replays `fnv1a32:41ae7f17` after the cure, equal to the hash recorded on the tape itself; the browser publishes no `secureWindowOpen` and falls back to `pendingSecure`, so its door does not move. GUARD `scripts/secure-choice-refusal.test.mjs` 2/2, added to `test:node-guards` (136), reads the word from the door rather than copying it, and covers all four master cases: refused with the word + bounded by the window's own milliseconds + defaulted; a lone choice still secures; nothing of the refusal reaches the tape (word, id, or a concatenated submission) and the reel still replays to its own hash; one parseable diagnostic line per refusal with stdout still strict NDJSON. GATES: tsc clean, build green, new guard 2/2, `skillmd-guard` 16/16 (grammar fence unchanged — no new union member, so nothing to regenerate), `same-game-audit` 3/3 + `same-game-report-guard` 3/3, `door-admission-ratchet` 1/1, `view-schema-guard` 3/3, `gr-sim.test` 24 tests 22 pass / 0 fail / 2 ruled skips, `standing-orders-module-split` 1/1, `no-emdash-guard` 1/1; e2e `ap16-6-browser-seat` + `tape-01-run-tape` **14 passed (13.9 s)** on both projects at one worker on port 5306, plus a plain-boot no-`?debug` probe CLEAN at 1280×800 and 390×844 (0 console, 0 page errors). Engine hash `60415d3f1be740e2cdb3236736adbe18bb9bf508f1c472ed17167725b590bae2` (era-5 guard reds until pinned — **the drain pins it**). CARRIED FINDINGS: **F-SCR-1** `package.json:20` — the last SEVEN guards of `test:node-guards` sit AFTER `&& node scripts/nul-audit.mjs`, which reads `process.argv` only for `--quiet` (`nul-audit.mjs:28`) and enumerates via `git ls-files`, so `e10-squall-scheduler`, `board-tape-gold`, `contract-hero-grit-override`, `run-tape-envelope-budget`, `e10-preserve-consumer`, `hero-move-verb` and `e8-regolith-gate-override` are inert arguments the battery never runs; a one-line move restores them, deliberately NOT done here (one concern per commit, and any red in them would be attributed to this slice). **F-SCR-2** `docs/bench/same-game-audit.md` — its citations are already stale on main (`MechanicsManifest.ts:970` regenerates to `:1007`, `View.ts:433` to `:447`, 8 files in a 2,096-line regen diff) and this slice rots two more (`StandingOrders.ts:437` → `:497`, `HeadlessContractSim.ts:1983` → `:2029`); the doc's guards are green and never diff it against its generator, so a regeneration is a separate hygiene commit. **F-SCR-3** the F-MCAP-1 wording "the refusal never reaching a transport that reads only views" was half right: gr-sim printed one stderr line per refusal all along; what was missing was the VIEW channel and the clock. GATE: drained when `git log main..fix/secure-choice-refusal-both-cures` is empty and the era pin carries `60415d3f…`.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:163:📉 **F-BUDGET-2 ANSWERED BY MEASUREMENT: NO MERGE GREW THE FIRST-TOWN TRANSFER — THE INSTRUMENT MOVES, NOT THE PAYLOAD; AND F-BUDGET-3 CURED: THE ADVANCE STREAM NO LONGER WARMS A CONTRACT WHILE THE TOWN IS STILL RAISING (Claude Opus 5 implementer, scratch worktree `wt-budget`, branch `fix/first-town-transfer-bisect`, 2026-09-06).** The deploy's own probe (`scripts/deploy.sh:95-:110`, reproduced command-for-command on scratch preview ports 5298+, `GR_RELEASE=e1` release build of each commit in a detached worktree) measures the BEFORE build `220886ab` at **21,609,438 / 21,609,438 bytes**, not the 12.0 / 15.6 MB F-BUDGET-2 attributes to it; `b31adf7b1`, `1119a4e90`, `87abff832`, `85900872f` and `3691960c` all measure the same, and current main `e5f3ac820` differs from `220886ab` by exactly ONE response — the town's random townsfolk pick (`townsfolk-assay-clerk.png` 256,713 B out, `townsfolk-tavernkeeper.png` 285,300 B in, net +28,587 B). **The four named merges moved it by zero bytes.** What moves it is the gate itself: three consecutive runs on ONE fixed build gave desktop 21,589,212 / **10,540,927** / 21,638,025 — a 2.05x swing on an identical bundle, with the low reading sitting beside the deploy's 12.0 MB; the same build measures 21,638,025 B on loopback and 6,411,798 B at an emulated 8 Mbps. The cue window ends at `data-asset-loading-state=ready`, which tracks only the scene's GLTF LoadingManager, so 208 character-sheet responses and two mp3s race that signal and a faster host counts more of them. The repo already said so: the two 2026-08-10 revisions of `artifacts/asset-diet/town-transfer-*.json` read 15,557,580 / 16,638,442 (`39fce97d9`) and 21,903,056 / 22,497,140 (`53b113b5d`), the whole "growth" a month before those merges existed. **OWED TO THE DRAIN: strike the F-BUDGET-2 row below (it names four innocent merges) — outside this task's firewall, so reported not edited.** THE ONE REAL LEAK, cured as F-BUDGET-3: in the town and run scenes the advance stream's priority-1 target is a CONTRACT, so entering the town pulled `the-claim-terrain.glb` + `the-claim-panorama.glb` (**1,052,408 B, 2 requests, issued at ~1.29 s while the town read `loading` from 0.43 s to 1.65 s**). `src/assets/AdvanceStream.ts` now re-arms a contract target on its own idle callback while the current scene reads `loading` (town target never held; fail-open on an absent attribute; no new published state). Contract prefetch during the raising: **1,052,408 B → 0 B**. The gated byte total is UNCHANGED and cannot change — the probe's window ends on the very signal the hold waits for — so the invariant is asserted as ORDER instead: `e2e/asset-diet.spec.ts` "the advance stream holds contract prefetch until the town is playable" (2 passed on the cure, 2 FAILED on the uncured main build, naming both GLBs). COMPOSITION, measured on the cure build, 21,638,025 B / 482 responses, both projects identical: character sheets **12,241,854 B (56.6%, 208 responses)** of which the hero's single runtime slot is 8,901,114 B in 141 · plates/portraits/UI 3,026,133 · audio 3,010,289 (two mp3s) · terrain+town plate 1,844,876 · town detail GLBs 1,487,316 · JS 27,557 B in **241 round trips**. RECOMMENDATIONS ONLY, not implemented: (1) split the hero slot's clips so `work8` 3,552,007 B + `attack8` 758,822 B stop loading for a town that never plays them (~4.3 MB, needs a warm-on-idle path or the first swing stalls); (2) defer the two mp3s to first playback (~3.0 MB, cheapest in the list, costs a bar of music on a cold link); (3) collapse the 241 per-cell `?url` modules into ~20 family manifests (`src/assets/SpriteAnimator.ts:170`, `src/assets/generated.ts:46`) — 27 KB of bytes but 241 round trips, which is what "veerrry slowly" feels like on a real link. GUARD: `scripts/first-town-request-families.test.mjs` (in `test:node-guards`) pins the window by sorted FAMILY allowlist, proven to bite. Evidence: `artifacts/first-town-transfer-bisect/` (per-build request list