tasks/BACKLOG.md:249:📊 **perf-optimization-survey READY-FOR-GATES → ✅ SHIPPED 7e85c9e43 (attended drain 2026-09-06 early; see the SHIPPED row above) (lane/b, 2026-09-05; Claude Opus 5 implementer, resumed from the codex run that died at 41 min on 'Selected model is at capacity') — SURVEY COMPLETE WITH NAMED GAPS, no game code changed.** Report `docs/reviews/2026-09-05-perf-survey.md`; instruments `scripts/perf-survey/*.mjs`; evidence `artifacts/perf-survey/**` (126 MB, largest file 4.5 MB); `node scripts/perf-survey/validate.mjs` PASSES on all 227 rows (schema + every published quantile re-derived from the raw per-frame samples + wave/contract/tier identity + tracked firewall). **Coverage 40 of 43 targets** (42 board contracts + town), 215 measured rows, **zero console/page/network errors**; the 12 failures are all F-PERF-11. **Two master premises corrected: the board holds 42 contracts, not 36** (the six the older count missed are the `terrainMesh: off` boards — four Deepwater, `e10-last-claim`, `e10-river`; the '29 of 36 prefer/require terrain' arithmetic survives against the larger denominator), **and every terrain grid is 128² = 32,768 triangles**, so a '64-grid' is a 4× cut, not 2×. 🚨 **EVERY FRAME TIMING IS UNTRUSTWORTHY AND LABELLED SO (F-PERF-1):** the host carried three other implementers, 1-minute load ran 3.3 → 114.0, 170 of 215 rows sat above load 12, and `frameMs` p50 is 8.3 ms everywhere (the headless rAF pace, not the game). The survey therefore ranks ONLY on structure. Findings: **F-PERF-3** the only draw-call budget (`perf-01`, `maxDrawCalls <= 200`) covers 1 map of 42 and `e9-seed-run` already hits max 211; **F-PERF-4** 63% of submitted triangles carry `frustumCulled = false` (72 sites in `src/`), and only 292 of 2,290 instance slots are inside the frustum on `the-claim` — `EnemyPool` submits 39,360 triangles of empty pool at wave 1 with `enemiesAlive: 0`; **F-PERF-5** estimated resident texture memory is 89–215 MB per map AFTER the shared-atlas merge, and six 1672×941 boss sheets are 50.3 MB of `the-claim`'s 166.8 MB; **F-PERF-8** 59% of the `county-e6-picnic` sim replay is `structuredClone`, 99.9% of it through `StandingOrders.snapshot()` deep-cloning the whole growing history (`src/agent/StandingOrders.ts:290`+`:845`); **F-PERF-7** 25 county tapes measured — **10 replay below 20× realtime** (worst `e10-last-claim` 8×; three of them on a genuinely quiet host at load 4-7, so contention does not explain them) and **6 more exceed the 60 s node ceiling entirely**, so the flagged set is at least 16 of 31; 🚨 **F-PERF-14 — CORRECTNESS, NOT SPEED, AND AHEAD OF THE WHOLE LADDER: `county-e3-moth-season` replays to a DIFFERENT event-log hash than the tape records** (`fnv1a32:9be0399e` vs `64e32dde`) and overruns its declared 10,801 ticks to 16,390 — reproduced twice at loads 64 and 16, and the repo's own `scripts/assay-replay-agent.mjs` produces the same divergent hash; all 24 other tapes match exactly and replay to their declared tick count. **The assayer verifies standings by that hash.** Stale reel or engine divergence: this survey does not resolve which, and files it as ladder rung 0 `moth-season-replay-hash-divergence`; **F-PERF-9/10** the town's first load is 23.99 MB of a 25 MB budget while `e5-regatta` is 31.49 MB, and the Fast-3G first frame is a fixed 7.25 s ± 0.02 on 0.89 MB (latency/parse-bound, so asset reduction moves none of it); **F-PERF-13** the same-document town round trip leaks 0.62 MB of JS heap per cycle with every three.js counter flat; **F-PERF-11** `e10-ember-shore`, `e10-archive-world` and `e10-river` silently fall back to `the-claim` (`fallbackReason: "unavailable-contract"`); **F-PERF-2** the `?debug` seam's own forced layout read costs ~320-350 ms per 6 s and is 0 ms in a plain boot — deliberately kept OFF the ladder as not-a-player-cost; **F-PERF-6** the per-system millisecond attribution did NOT resolve (noise-dominated) and is reported as failed, not as no-effect. 🎯 **§1b IS THE REFRAME: a late quiet window (load ≤ 12, median of three, 3 clean samples per arm, dirty samples discarded and retaken) got nine clean arms across five maps — the-claim, e9-seed-run (the 210-call map), e2-hill-mine, e1-night-shift (156k triangles), e4-long-road — and EVERY ONE delivers 119-120 fps with 0.9-1.3 ms render-submit CPU and 0-1 long frames per 10 s.** Triangle count varies 60% and draw calls 3.3× across those arms without moving the number. **There is NO desktop frame problem; the render-side rungs are headroom / GPU-memory / low-end-device arguments and say so.** The costs that are large on the hardware actually measured are the sim, delivery and resident texture memory. Two arms (town, e1-night-shift stress) never got three clean samples and are reported as insufficient, not filled in. §10 is the top-ten table and §11 the ladder (rung 0 = F-PERF-14, then 10 proposed masters, each with the number it must move and its gate; five gates are structural and stand without a quiet host). §8 cross-references the Astra wave: asset-diet manifest, shared atlas, bounded prefetch, triangle sampler and the Lantern stage are all IN the measured build `5f1cc3cea` and are not re-litigated; F-PERF-4 is the off-camera census F-ASTRA-6 asked for; **landmark-lighting-calibration (`ab3704716`) is NOT in the measured build**. GAPS NAMED: the census's own frame timings (partly closed by §1b; no lite/mobile arm re-taken clean), ms attribution, 3 of 6 attribution maps, 3 unlaunchable contracts, 6 county tapes past the 60 s ceiling, GPU-side allocation, the leaking module, and any real hardware. 🚨 **HOST CONDITION AT HANDOFF (23:47): the disk is 100% FULL — 117 MB free of 3.6 TB — which is host-wide and affects every implementer on this box, not just this lane.** Nothing was deleted (retention law); the survey's CPU profiles are left untracked on disk rather than adding ~70 MB of git objects to a full volume.
