tasks/BACKLOG.md:403:🧾🔴 **F-2477-1 — THE RUNNER ANNOUNCES ITS OWN EVIDENCE LOSSES OUT LOUD, AND NOTHING HAS EVER READ THEM: 55 DISTINCT WITHHELD PATHS ARE LOST, AND THE LOSS IS ASYMMETRIC BETWEEN THE TWO RIGS OF A HEAT (s2477, 2026-09-03, NINTH CONSECUTIVE DRY BOARD — the general form of F-2476-1, which s2476 priced as the next fire's work).** ⚙️ **MECHANISM: `lane-runner-v3.sh:115–116` commits a lane's DELTA, so a path already dirty in the lane's baseline is not that run's to claim — it withholds it and logs `[lane-runner-v3] withheld baseline-dirty path: <path>`, once per path. The refusal is CORRECT for source (claiming another task's uncommitted edits is the cross-contamination the delta-commit exists to prevent) and INVERTED for `artifacts/**` evidence, where it drops the run's own evidence on the floor while the run reports success.** 📊 **MEASURED s2477 over 341 run logs: `5` runs withheld `112` rows / `58` distinct paths — `3` TRACKED, `0` on any other ref, `0` on disk in the main worktree, in any of the four lanes, in any `-salvage` tree or under `worktrees/art`, and **`55` LOST**.** 🎯 **THE MATERIAL LOSS IS `artifacts/gauntlet-heat10-r2-20260902/` (54 of the 55), AND ITS SHAPE IS WHAT EARNS THIS A ROW: a heat is a two-rig COMPARISON, and only one side survived. `prime/` is COMPLETE — 66 files, all four maps. `pi/` kept only `e1-baron` (15) and `e2-hill-mine` (11); **`pi/the-claim/` and `pi/e1-night-shift/` are gone entirely** — two of the four rows the note's own PI matrix reports, including its "+1 wave on Night Shift" headline and its "lost its prior Claim row to a controller bug at the secure window" claim. Also gone: **BOTH charters** (`pi/charter.md`, `prime/charter.md` — the note's own fairness control, *"separate charters, state, matrices, and notebooks"*), **`preflight.md`** (the mandatory preflight its arena-law section cites), and the whole **`probe/`** set (the early probe accepted at rank 5, hash `fnv1a32:8886f412`). The rig that came off worse in the write-up is the one whose transcripts are missing.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: `heat10-r2-note.md` is TRACKED and survives with every number intact, so no CONCLUSION was lost — only its SUBSTANTIATION — and this is NOT a false green, because nothing ever reported health about these bytes. Nothing can be recovered: the bytes are in no object database and on no disk. The owed act is the JUDGEMENT, recorded here, not a backfill.** 🕳️ **WHY NO INSTRUMENT SAW IT, verified by READING rather than inferred: `review-evidence-audit` is CITATION-DRIVEN over `reviews/*.md`, so a heat note under `artifacts/` that cites its own evidence is never a subject (its live `ABSENT=17` contains none of these); `art-staging-audit` scans art staging and declares so; and the withheld lines sit in `tasks/runs/*.log`, which no instrument reads. Three honest tools, three orthogonal keys, one hole nobody could report — F-2357-1 exactly.** 🛠️ **CURE: `scripts/withheld-evidence-audit.mjs` — reads the ANCHORED withheld lines and classifies every path `TRACKED` / `IN-GIT` / `ON-DISK` / `LOST`, declares its corpus on stdout ALWAYS including the happy path (F-2208-1), anchors its root to `import.meta.url` so a subdirectory cannot silently narrow it (F-2220-1), REFUSES rather than classifying against an empty tracked set (F-2215-1), bounds every spawn at 240 s with `SIGKILL` (F-2430-1), and prints the `git add -f` remedy BESIDE the alarm for the one bucket that can still be acted on (F-2451-1's measured lesson: the tool that prints its remedy is acted on in minutes, the one that does not is reported correctly and ignored for 385 fires). Advisory, exit 0 by default; `--strict` gives 1 = owed, 2 = could not answer. Named in `scripts/fire.md` beside the F-2357-1/F-2389-1 evidence reads, so it has a reader at the moment it applies.** ⏱️ **THE OPERATIONAL HALF, and it is where the value is: RUN IT AT DRAIN TIME, not only on a dry board. `ON-DISK` is RECOVERABLE and `LOST` is not, and the only thing between them is whether the lane worktree still holds the bytes — s2476 recovered its own two screenshots for exactly that reason, and a `git clean -fd` or a lane reset in between is what turned these 54 into `LOST`.** ⛔ **ANCHOR THE NEEDLE ON THE LINE PREFIX IF YOU GREP IT BY HAND: run logs EMBED diff hunks and grep output, so they QUOTE the runner's own printf. Measured: a bare substring key matches `6` files, the anchored key `5` — the sixth withheld nothing and only narrates the emitter. That is F-2102-1's `FIRE END` defect on a new key, and it sends a fire chasing a run that never lost anything. The bare key is what s2476 handed forward, and anchoring it was the first thing this fire did.** 🔒 **Guarded by `scripts/withheld-evidence-audit-guard.test.mjs` — 11 arms, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`, `gate-caller-audit` PASS.** 🔬 **TEETH, seven variants manufactured on scratch copies, and the sweep doubled as a reachability audit (s2226): EVERY arm reds under at least one valid variant — drop the anchor reds 1,2,6,9 · never-refuse-on-empty-tracked-set reds 10 ALONE · declare-only-on-failure reds 3 ALONE · collapse strict 2 into 1 reds 4,8 · strict-never-non-zero reds 4,5,8 · re-anchor the root to `process.cwd()` reds 7 ALONE · skip the lane worktrees reds 11 ALONE. **The sweep FOUND A DECORATION AND THAT IS ITS WHOLE VALUE: the lane-worktree branch — the very branch deciding `ON-DISK` vs `LOST`, i.e. recoverable vs not — was reddened by NOTHING until arm 11 was built for it.**** ⚠️ **ONE METHOD NOTE, PAID FOR IN THIS FIRE AND WORTH MORE THAN THE CURE: my first sweep reported a variant as *"reds all 10 arms"*, and the edit had produced INVALID JAVASCRIPT. A variant that cannot parse is a CONSTRUCTION REFUSAL, not evidence about the defect — it fails LOUD, which is correct behaviour, but scoring it as coverage would have credited every arm with teeth it had not earned and hidden the one real decoration. **Assert your variant RAN before you read what it reddened** — this streak's vacuous-control trap (F-2215-1) wearing yet another costume, on the variant side rather than the control side.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is F-2357-1's orthogonal-axes lesson one rung down: those two instruments partition on WHERE a file lives and WHO CITES it. This one partitions on WHO DECLINED TO COMMIT IT — a key neither can express, and the only key under which this loss is visible at all. When a component REFUSES to do something and says so, that refusal is a SIGNAL WITH AN OWNER; ask who reads it, and if the answer is nobody, you have found a hole no census keyed on corpora or handlers can see.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:407:🧹✅ **GATE-WORKTREE SWEEP — 100 GB RECLAIMED, AND FIVE ORPHANED COMMITS CAUGHT ON THE WAY OUT (s2475).** The seven leftover gate worktrees s2474 measured and left as its priority (C) are removed; the disk went **166 Gi → 266 Gi free (96% → 93%)**, free inodes 1.7G → 2.8G. ✅ **s2474’s dirt measurement REPRODUCES EXACTLY, re-measured rather than inherited (Mistake #4): 99 tracked-dirt lines across all seven, **every one `M` (modified)** — zero deletions, zero additions — confined to `reviews/shots-*/` (64) and `artifacts/` (35), with **ZERO source-class paths** and zero `reviews/*.md`. Every dirty file is TRACKED, so main holds a committed copy of each and the worktree merely carried a re-rendered one; untracked content was `node_modules` plus `logs/guard-stats.jsonl`, which F-2398-2 forbids tracking anyway.** 🕳️ **F-2475-1 — WHAT THAT MEASUREMENT COULD NOT SEE, AND IT IS THE REASON THIS IS A FINDING RATHER THAN A CHORE: s2474 offered the sweep as “a one-command sweep for whoever wants the disk back” on the strength of WORKING-TREE dirt alone, and a dirt census is structurally blind to what the HEAD holds.** 📊 **MEASURED s2475 with `git for-each-ref --contains <tip>`: FIVE of the seven detached tips (`gate-s2326` `d824ecb1b`, `gate-s2395` `b9067b2f0`, `gate-s2442` `e73b48966`, `gate-s2460` `250718b11`, `gate-s2466` `e0e24ed48`) were **held by ZERO named refs and are NOT ancestors of main** — the worktree HEAD was the only thing keeping them reachable. The other two (`gate-s2461`, `gate-s2471`) are on main and held by 10 and 6 refs respectively.** ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: realised cost ZERO — nothing was lost, no fire ran the bare sweep, and every one of the five tips is a SPENT trial merge whose content did land (`kit-guard-generic-damage` for instance reads `status="merged"` at `adacf5e13`, asked of `drain-block-check` rather than assumed). This is NOT undrained work.** 🎯 **What earns it a row is the DIRECTION: the offered command would have dropped the last ref on five commits, and the RETENTION LAW’s whole subject is factory history leaving the disk with nobody noticing — a `git worktree remove` reports success identically either way.** 🛠️ **CURE, and it is the house pattern already written for exactly this shape (`lane-usable --cure`: archive the tip BEFORE resetting, history first): each unheld tip was archived to `archive/gate-s<N>-s2475-<sha8>` and **all five refs were verified to resolve to their exact 40-char tip BEFORE any worktree was touched** (F-2215-1 — a control that does not assert its own validity cannot be told from the silence it measures). Removal is therefore fully reversible: `git worktree add <path> archive/gate-s<N>-s2475-<sha8>` reconstructs any of them byte-for-byte, which is what makes this an ARCHIVE under §4.10 rather than a deletion.** ✅ **POST-SWEEP VERIFICATION: `git worktree list` holds 97 entries and ZERO `gate-*`; main’s working tree is byte-unchanged from fire start; all four lanes re-read at their expected state and lane-c’s LIVE runner was undisturbed throughout (its run log grew 3,400,063 → 3,434,583 B across the sweep).** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a new axis for this factory’s long-running question: twenty-odd fires have asked *how can this subject set come back EMPTY?* This asks the sibling — **how can a CLEAN measurement be clean about the wrong object?** A worktree is TWO things, a checkout and a ref, and “nothing uncommitted here” is a complete answer about the first and says nothing whatever about the second. When you inherit a safety verdict, enumerate what the measurement RANGED OVER, not merely what it concluded.**
