tasks/BACKLOG.md:5131:- ✍️ **s1221 — E2 RAIL-TOUGH-ONLY BIND AUTHORED + QUEUED → lane-a** (`tasks/lane-a-e2-rail-tough-only-bind.md`; leaf `e2-rail-tough-only-bind` registered in the **same commit** `a615f8e9`; `drain-block-check --queue` **✅ CLEAR**; full `run-guards` **8/8** incl. `test:task-guards` rc=0; `test:node-guards` **74/74 rc=0** on main; `CODEX:` at **column 0, line 1**; `lane/m3` re-verified a **false-ahead SAFE DUPE** immediately before the `cp` — 2 ahead at `f44097b1` but `git diff --diff-filter=A main..lane/m3` is **EMPTY**, its two commits are the already-shipped `94dd863b` + `b5be7ab3`, and the only file that differs (`src/agent/ToolSurface.ts`) differs because **main moved ahead**; lane worktree clean). **THE THREE-SHEET WIRING SLICE STAYS STOPPED — THIS BINDS THE ONE SHEET WHOSE ROWS ARE SETTLED.** `f61843c0` shipped as an explicit **PARTIAL** (2 rows landed, 2 parked), so Coal Thief row 0 and Steam Wrecker row 1 are **still wrong on main** (F-1193-3) and F-1188-2 forbids binding them; Rail Tough is the only sheet whose four rows have all been addressed. 🔑 **F-1221-1 (MEASURED THIS FIRE, NOT INHERITED) — THE COMMITTED INSTRUMENT IS RED ON MAIN AND ITS PROVENANCE CLAIM IS VOID.** `node logs/s1190-rail-tough-wrench-probe.mjs` throws **`Mirror-floor control drifted: 0.4585250692698196`** against its pinned `0.452` (`:246`), before writing anything — so the byte-identity of `artifacts/eight-winds-rail-tough/probe-results.json` that s1191 published **cannot be reproduced on today's tree**. ✓ **The decisive control was built and run** (`logs/s1221-rail-tough-row1-repair-control.mjs` — the **same instrument**, s1190 verbatim through its `results` object, with only the diagonal sheet path made an argv parameter and the assertions truncated): against the pre-batch sheet (`git show f61843c0^:…`) `mirrorFloor` = **0.45249519682907424**, against today's = **0.4585250692698196**, and **row 0 (`−69.5 −63.5 −52.8 −56.5`), row 2 (`52.6 59.8 46.4 53.7`) and row 3 (`59.3 64.5 58.3 61.2`) wrench offsets are numerically IDENTICAL across the two sheets while row 1 moved (`−62.4 −45.5 −56.7 −52.7` → `−41.4 −52.2 −54.6 −47.1`)**. ⇒ **the drift is caused by row 1 alone — the art moved under a pinned constant; the instrument is not decaying.** 🔑 **F-1221-2 — THE SPEC'S ORDERING SAFETY-NET DOES NOT EXIST ON THE PATH THIS SLICE USES.** §4 warns that wiring before extracting makes a block fail *"as a whole"* and fall back — but `SpriteAnimator.ts:979-984` applies `walkSheetHasProcessedCells` to **`walk8` only** and returns `walk4` with **no check at all**, and the E2 slots have **no `walk8` block** (`characters.v2.json:196-211`). A missing cell then resolves to `null` at `loadProcessedTexture:1148-1149` — **no throw, no log: a silently absent texture for exactly the four new headings.** ⚠️ **HENCE SCOPE 1 IS A HARD STOP GATE, AND A STOP IS A SUCCESS.** F-1188-2 measured rows 0/1 at **+0.119 DIRECT-dominant** (the two front rows reading as **one** heading) — the defect the batch regenerated row 1 to cure — and **`reviews/eight-winds-e2-row-repairs.md` publishes no post-repair heading measurement of Rail Tough at all** (its pixel gates are Steam Wrecker cyan clusters + 12-row byte-exactness; row 1 appears only as *"it moved"*). The wrench control that **does** still pass is a **front/back** tell and carries no east/west information — F-1191-1's own warning that *"the `nw`/`ne` LABELS are one tier below the PAIR"*. A wrong mapping renders the character walking backwards and **no test catches it**. 📋 **The master also forecloses the trap that would make a correct edit do nothing:** the slot's `aliases` map (`{se:e, ne:e, sw:w, nw:w}`) **overwrites** explicit directions for every non-hero slot (`SpriteAnimator.ts:843` guards only `charHero`), so it must be **emptied for this slot** — and the spec's alternative (widening that guard) is **refused by name**, because it is a contract-wide `src/` behaviour change that would silently activate every other slot's half-built diagonals.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5132:- ✍️ **s1220 — LB-03 BENCH SEED SETS AUTHORED + QUEUED → lane-b** (`tasks/lane-b-lb-03-bench-seed-sets.md`; leaf `lb-03-bench-seed-sets` registered in the **same commit** `54c684fd`; `drain-block-check` **✅ CLEAR**; `goal-tracker` **2/2**; full `run-guards` **8/8** incl. `test:task-guards` rc=0; `CODEX:` at **column 0, line 1**; lane-b proved a **SAFE DUPE by blob identity** before the `cp` — `lane/m4` is 3 ahead but `functions/api/standings.ts` `bc0c29f9`, `e2e/lb-01-county-standings.spec.ts` `74f0100b`, `src/game/Game.ts` `5b53586d`, `src/encyclopedia/reader.{ts,css}` are **byte-identical on both refs**, i.e. LB-01+LB-02 landed path-scoped and the branch was never fast-forwarded; worktree clean). 🔑 **THE FINDING THAT MADE THIS AUTHORABLE: the ratified law has had no mechanism since the day it was written.** `specs/agent-play/README.md:61` says *"bench runs use a frozen seed set per contract (comparability)"* — but `Game.ts:5509-5510`+`:5536` decides the mode as `pinnedSeed === null ? 'live' : 'bench'`, so **`seedMode:'bench'` is asserted by the mere PRESENCE of a `?seed=` param, against no set at all**, and `standings.ts:139`/`:143` accepts the claim with no membership check. Sharpest illustration: `DebugParams.ts:41-42` makes `?bench=fullbase` inject seed `'perf-02-fullbase'` **and `timescale: 24`**, so **a perf-harness run at 24× speed that secures submits itself as a BENCH row** into the corpus the owner intends to compare models on. ⚠️ **AND THE OBVIOUS IMPLEMENTATION IS A TRAP THE MASTER FORECLOSES:** putting `benchSeeds` on the contract manifest would make the frozen set **player-editable in the Charter Press** — `contractDescriptorJson` is `JSON.stringify(contract,null,2)` over the **whole object** (`ContractFamilies.ts:886`), `normalizeContractDescriptor:1480` shape-checks round-trips through it (`CharterStamp.ts:40-42`), and `DescriptorInspector.ts:347` enumerates descriptor fields **generically** into the editor. **The set therefore lives in its own `assets/contracts/bench-seeds.json`, and no `contracts.json` is touched** — a real single source of truth, since client (`ContractFamilies.ts:2-21`) and server (`standings.ts:1-10`) already import the same ten bundles. 🔓 **Server-side rejection of a non-member bench claim is argued SAFE from a read, not assumed:** `DebugParams.ts:34` returns `DEFAULT_PARAMS` **before** the URL is parsed when `RELEASE_E1`, so `getDebugSeed()` is unconditionally `null` in the release build ⇒ **no release client can emit `bench` at all** ⇒ F-1216-2's split-deploy hazard cannot bite. The runner is ordered to re-verify that itself and STOP if any path contradicts it. 📋 **TWO THINGS DELIBERATELY NOT SETTLED, both flagged to the owner rather than invented:** (1) **how a bench run is ever LAUNCHED in a release build** — fact 3's corollary is that bench mode is currently *unreachable* in the shipped game, and adding a door is a debug-door-in-release **design fork**; (2) the **reversible ruling** that an arbitrary **non-member** seed submits **nothing** (labelling it `live` would pollute the live board with pinned-seed runs), implemented as one commented early return so a one-word veto reverses it.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5133:- ✅ **`standing-orders-wave-early-seam` SHIPPED `a0aae8764829c8794dbf0423d8b433216acc1b05`** (s1222 drain, review `reviews/standing-orders-wave-early-seam.md`; goal leaf `standing-orders-wave-early-seam` flipped `queued`→`merged` in the ledger commit; done-move `drained-s1222-a0aae876-…`). 🔑 **THE INHERITED FLAKE WAS A DETERMINISTIC SPEC-SEQUENCING BUG, AND `StandingOrders.ts` WAS NEVER AT FAULT.** With `?nowaves&timescale=8` the spec could let the scheduled wave countdown **expire** before calling `startWaveForTest(1)`; resetting the timer to 30 s **cannot make an already-expired expectation early**, so `wave_early` never fired and the assertion at `:121` was correctly rejecting the transition. Runner’s captured deciding tick: desktop `at=76.2667`, mobile `at=61.6`, both `expectedWaveAt=30.2667`, countdown `0→30`, guard **false**. **Cure is test-only** (freeze manual sim → `setWave(0)` → install orders → one fixed tick → force wave 1 → one fixed tick → resume) **plus a new seam test that deliberately expires the countdown first**, so the mode cannot return silently. ✓ **`git show --name-only 6999e57a -- src/ functions/ assets/ package.json playwright.config.ts` is EMPTY** — none of the four cures (timeout / retry / worker config / product source) that would have hidden the bug instead of fixing it. **Gates on the MERGED tree:** tsc clean · build **1.08 s** · `--list` **2466/344** (LB-03’s 2464 + 1 test × 2 projects) · run-guards **8/8** · plain-boot probe **CLEAN** desktop **and** 390px. ⚖️ **THE TWO ARMS, COUNTED PER PROJECT AND NEVER POOLED** (s1221’s standing warning): **TREATMENT** `Running 32 tests using 4 workers` → **desktop 16/0, mobile 16/0, exit 0, under 1-min loadavg 25.28 with lane-a live**; **CONTROL** (main’s pre-cure blob `6a68e219`, else identical) `Running 8 tests using 4 workers` → **desktop 0/4, mobile 0/4, exit 1**, every failure the same test at `:80`. **0/8 → 32/32 across the single blob under test.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5135:- ✅ **F-1223-1 (s1223) — F-1222-3’s REMEDY IS DISCHARGED, AND ITS OWN PREMISE AND ITS OWN NUMBER BOTH NEEDED CORRECTING ON THE WAY.** The ordered sweep was *“strike the label from task-master prose; it is not in `logs/suite-red-inventory.md` (checked)”*. **Both halves of that were wrong, in opposite directions.** 🔑 **(a) THE SOURCE WAS NEVER SWEPT, BECAUSE IT WAS GREPPED FOR UNDER THE WRONG KEY.** `logs/suite-red-inventory.md:629` is where *“25% mobile-only at w4”* was **minted** — s1216’s measured table, `| e2e/ap-standing-orders.spec.ts:80 | mobile-chrome | 0/8 | 0/8 | 2/8 (25.0%) | monotonic |`. The inventory keys the subject on **`:80`** (the test declaration); the task masters copied it as **`:121`** (the assertion), and one master as **`:115`→`:156`**. A grep for `:121` therefore cleared the very file the number came from. ➡️ **Correcting only the copies would have left the source able to regenerate them** — so the fix was applied to the class: **four masters** (`lane-a-e2-rail-tough-only-bind`, `lane-b-lb-03-bench-seed-sets`, `lane-c-ap-06b-panel-ladder-and-voice`, `lane-c-agent-rung-honest-gate`) **plus `tasks/ap-orders-adapter-wiring.md`’s 🔴 block** (which actively instructed a runner to expect the red and not to trust its own green), **plus a SUPERSEDED section appended to the inventory itself** — appended, never rewritten: the s1216 row is honest history and states its own denominator. 🔬 **(b) “100% IN BOTH PROJECTS” IS ITSELF A LOAD SAMPLE — the same mistake one level down.** F-1222-3 replaced a rate quoted without its load with *another* rate quoted without its load. An **interleaved T–C–T** control on main (pre-cure blob `6a68e219`, restored byte-identically; `git diff HEAD` empty) measured **desktop 2/4 (50.0%) and mobile 2/4 (50.0%) at loadavg 5.4→15.7**, every failure the real assertion at `:121`. With s1216 and s1222 that is a clean monotonic **load-response curve, symmetric across projects at every point**: **~12.5% quiet → 50%/50% at loadavg ~15 → 100%/100% at loadavg ~25.** ✓ **“Mobile-only” is not merely refuted, it is quantified as unremarkable:** at the 12.5% rate s1216 measured, a desktop arm drawing 0/8 has probability 0.875⁸ ≈ **34%** — a coin-flip, not a project-specific defect. ✅ **(c) THE CURE HOLDS, AND IS NOW THE BEST-EVIDENCED THING IN THIS THREAD: 64/64 GREEN** across two independent fires and trees — s1222 32/32 at loadavg 25.28, s1223 **T1 16/16** (loadavg →14.50) and **T2 16/16 at loadavg →29.06**, the heaviest load this subject has ever been measured under. **It is no longer a known red: a failure there is now a real regression**, and all five documents say so. ⚠️ **(d) THE CONTROL I NEARLY BELIEVED.** My first control arm returned a tidy **8/8 red, exit 1** — and was **contaminated**: every failure was `ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED`, because the scratch dev server had silently died (spawned detached with **piped** stdio, so it was killed by a full 64 KB pipe buffer once its parent exited). **It agreed with the conclusion it existed to test, and only the error text gave it away.** Discarded, root-caused, the helper fixed to log to a file (`logs/session-scratch/s1223-serve.mjs`), and the arm re-run. **THE LESSON: a corrective inherits the authority of the finding that ordered it, and that is exactly why it must be re-derived — F-1222-3 was right about the disease and wrong about where the infection lived. And a control that fails for the wrong reason is not a control; it is a second rumour, wearing the uniform of evidence.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5136:- ✅ **E2 RAIL-TOUGH-ONLY BIND SHIPPED `7ed00040cc828d37191375076690dd912d22fea5`** (s1224 drain, review `reviews/e2-rail-tough-only-bind.md`, leaf `e2-rail-tough-only-bind` → `merged`). **The eight-winds ladder lands its first E2 enemy, and it landed because a gate that was allowed to cancel the task said yes.** Scope 1 required proof that the sheet’s *repaired* front rows are a lawful mirror pair before any binding — a STOP there would have been a success, exactly as slice 3’s was. The runner measured it: **`0v1` wrench-excluded delta `-0.118521`** against F-1188-2’s **|0.095|** noise ceiling, with the already-settled **`2v3` pair reproduced as a positive control at `-0.127001`**, and **all nine declared shaft/jaw mask sizes mirror-dominant on both arms** (`-0.114271…-0.121988` and `-0.117634…-0.127001`). ➡️ **Bound `sw`/`se`/`nw`/`ne` on `char.e2.rail_tough.walk4` ONLY, and emptied that slot’s alias map** — `{ "se":"e","ne":"e","sw":"w","nw":"w" }` → `{}`. That is the trap that makes a correct edit do nothing: the alias pass runs **after** explicit directions and `SpriteAnimator.ts:843` exempts only `charHero`, so the aliases would have overwritten the new bind. The runner emptied the one slot rather than widening the `src/` guard — surgical and reversible, as the master demanded. **Steam Wrecker and Coal Thief are byte-untouched** (F-1193-3 still open, and binding either would ship the exact defect slice 3 stopped to prevent). ✓ **Gates RE-MEASURED on the merged tree, not copied from the runner:** tsc clean · build green (`✓ built in 1.61s`, asset diet `1099906/1500000`) · **node-guards 78/78** · `--list` **2468 tests in 345 files** (+2/+1 = the new spec × 2 projects) · own spec **2/2 both projects** · plain boot **zero console/page errors desktop AND 390 px**, asserted inside the new no-`?debug` spec · **zero `src/` bytes**. 🔢 **The guard count is the tell that re-measuring matters:** the runner read **76/76** against a **74** baseline — its own stale lane base — while main’s baseline had become **76** after s1222 added `bench-seeds.test.mjs`. The merged tree must read **78**, and does; both guards survived the one-file `package.json` 3-way graft. ⚠️ **The lane base `dddbb7e7` predates three merges, so `main..lane/m3` displays ~29,000 phantom deletions** (`bench-seeds.json`, `standings.ts`, two review files, the entire `logs/session-scratch/` tree). Classified against the **merge-base** instead: 29 lane-touched files, exactly **one** also moved by main.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5137:- 🔴 **F-1224-3 (s1224, non-blocking, RECORDED) — `e2e/wire-e2-enemy-walk4.spec.ts:15` IS A BASELINE RED ON MAIN IN BOTH PROJECTS AND IS IN NO TABLE ANYWHERE.** Surfaced by the rail-tough drain’s adjacent battery and then *attributed* rather than assumed: a control on **pre-merge main** (detached worktree at `79fb8fa2`, served on scratch port **5236** because 5188 is shared with the live lane worktrees, and **validated over HTTP before use** — it serves the old contract with no `walkdiag4` cells) reproduced it **2/2**, matching the treatment’s **2/2**. **4/4 across two trees and two loads.** The spec’s E2 roster/motion half passes; the failure is its unrelated **E1** control timing out after five seconds on `char.bandit_base.loaded`. **Recorded as an APPENDED ADDENDUM to `logs/suite-red-inventory.md`, not as a table row** — those tables are generated by `scripts/suite-red-inventory.mjs` from a single whole-suite run and carry a reduction check that counts their own rows, so hand-inserting would falsify the artifact and its self-check together. **The cost of leaving it unrecorded is concrete: this drain spent a full control battery proving a red it did not cause, and the next E2-adjacent battery would have paid that same cost again.** Diagnosis of the E1 loader timeout is a separate slice.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5138:- 🔬 **F-1224-4 (s1224, non-blocking, PROCESS) — THE RUNNER’S ADJACENT-SUITE SELECTION MISSED THE SPEC NAMED FOR ITS OWN SUBJECT.** It ran `eight-winds-hero`, `lane-c-activations-assay-office` and `wire-e2-enemy-walk4` — but **not `e2e/e2-enemies.spec.ts`, whose own test title is *“wave pulses spawn **Rail Toughs**, Steam Wreckers, and Coal Thieves from E2 data”***. That spec carries **four** reds in both projects. Nothing shipped wrong — all four are pre-existing and already in the inventory at `:125` and `:317` — **but the battery was blind exactly where the slice was most likely to break something.** ➡️ **Corrective: derive adjacent-suite selection MECHANICALLY** — `grep -rl` the subject’s identifiers (`railtough`, `rail_tough`, `characters.v2`) across `e2e/` returns the eight specs this drain ran, and it costs one command. Choosing adjacent suites by hand is choosing them by memory, and memory is what the firewall lists exist to replace. 🔎 **A corroboration of F-1224-1 fell out of this:** the inventory keys those reds on their **assertion** lines (`:125`, `:317`) while playwright reports their **declaration** lines (`:113`, `:314`) — the same one-subject-two-coordinates split that let the `ap-standing-orders` label survive a sweep for three fires.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5139:- 🔬 **F-1224-1 (s1224) — THE CLASS s1223 DIAGNOSED IS NOW MEASURED: 38 of 300 forward-looking `spec:line` citations (12.7%) NO LONGER NAME THE TEST THEY WERE WRITTEN FOR, AND 32 OF THOSE CARRY NO TITLE TO RECOVER THE SUBJECT FROM.** F-1223-1 concluded that known-reds prose *“cite[s] reds by LINE NUMBER, which drifts”* and recommended keying on spec + test title instead — then fixed **one subject’s five documents** and left the class uncounted. **Counted now** (`logs/session-scratch/s1224-citation-drift-audit.mjs`; report and raw JSON committed beside it): every `e2e/*.spec.ts:<line>` citation under `tasks/**` was resolved to its enclosing test title **twice** — once in the spec as it stood at the commit that INTRODUCED the citation (`git log -S`), once on today’s main — and the two compared. **516 citations across 1728 files; 216 sit in the untracked `tasks/done/` + `tasks/failed/` archive and are unmeasurable by construction** (untracked files have no `git log`), leaving **300 forward-looking: 262 STABLE, 38 DRIFTED.** Of the 38, **31 are SEVERE** (the coordinate now lands *inside a different named test*, so a reader gets a confidently wrong answer) and **7 are GAP** (it lands between tests — visibly broken, therefore harmless). ⚖️ **THE HARM IS MUCH NARROWER THAN THE RATE, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT:** 15 of the 20 masters carrying number-only drift are `merged` or `superseded` goal leaves — dead prose the RETENTION LAW protects and no fire will queue. **The live surface is `tasks/BACKLOG.md` itself (8 number-only drifted citations) plus the owner’s-desk draft (F-1224-2).** ➡️ **THIS ANSWERS THE OWNER’S-DESK QUESTION s1223 PUT UP** (*“should known-reds blocks cite reds by spec + test-title instead of line number?”*) **with a measurement instead of an intuition: of the 38 drifted citations only 6 also quoted a still-valid test title and were therefore self-healing; 32 had nothing but the rotted coordinate.** The recommendation stands and is now evidenced — **cite the title; the line number is a convenience that decays** — and the cheap half of it needs no shape ruling at all, because it is not a schema: *a citation that carries its test title survives its line number moving.* ⚠️ **I REFUTED MY OWN INSTRUMENT ONCE, AND ONLY BECAUSE I HAND-CHECKED FIRST.** The title-recovery measure initially scored the `mp-02-lockstep` entry **number-only** — but I had already read that entry by hand and watched it quote *“hash mismatch … restores from relay snapshot”*. The measure was matching exact substrings and the ledger had **abbreviated the title with an ellipsis**; switching to a ≥4-word contiguous-run match reclassified it as self-healing and raised the recovered count from 3 to 6. **A hand-verified positive is the only thing that catches an instrument which under-reports — the automated pass agreed with itself perfectly while being wrong.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5141:- ♻️ **F-1224-2 (s1224, CORRECTED IN THE SAME COMMIT) — THE ONE LIVE OWNER’S-DESK ITEM RESTING ON DRIFTED POINTERS: ALL THREE OF ITS COORDINATES HAD ROTTED, AND ALL THREE OF ITS CONCLUSIONS SURVIVED RE-DERIVATION.** The `tasks/DRAFT-e1-trail-guide-plain-boot-proof.md` desk item — a *go/hold* the owner is asked to answer in one word — argued its scope from three pointers into `e2e/trail-guide.spec.ts`. On today’s main: **`:113` is a blank line inside *“fresh profile sees the first three trail beats once, in order, and reload stays quiet”*** and **`:135` is `await approachFirstSeam(page, 0.2);` inside *“first build-menu open teaches once…”*** — neither proves the greenhorn offer they were cited for; and **`:42` is `page: Page,`, a parameter of the shared `openRun` helper, not “its test”.** ✓ **But every claim the item makes is TRUE, re-derived at source:** the greenhorn offer *is* proven on genuinely plain boots, at **`:201`** *“first boot asks the greenhorn question once and sets the preset”* (`page.goto` of `/`, storage cleared) and **`:223`** *“profile-title first boot offers the same greenhorn choice once”* (`/?profiles`) — so *“~40% narrower than its draft claims”* **holds**; and the bark-beat track *is* unproven in a plain launch, because its test at **`:100`** boots through `openRun` (`:41`) whose default path (`:43`) carries `?debug&contract=the-claim&nospawn&nolevel` — so that half **holds too**. **The desk item has been edited in place to carry the verified pointers** (the stale ones are preserved in this entry, per the RETENTION LAW) **and the owner’s call is unchanged — it simply now rests on coordinates that are true.** 💡 **THE SHAPE WORTH REMEMBERING: a conclusion can outlive the evidence that supports it.** Checking the pointers alone would have condemned a sound argument; re-deriving the subject rescued it. **Verify the citation, but re-derive the claim — they fail independently.**
