tasks/BACKLOG.md:5214:🟡 **F-1336-4** (s1336, MEASURED — TWO DESK ROWS ARE ONE OWNER DECISION, AND THE CHEAPER-LOOKING ONE HAS A CLAUSE THAT IS NARROWLY FALSE). F-1313-3 asks the owner for one word — *(a)* revoked = the game won't do it for you but the agent may, or *(b)* revoked = the ability is unavailable to anyone — and F-1313-2's fork **(4)**, *"whether consent should gate direct tool calls at all"*, **is the same question**. Answering **(b)** collapses both rows. ✓ BOTH RE-VERIFIED AT SOURCE THIS FIRE AND UNCHANGED SINCE FILING: `src/agent/ToolSurface.ts:233-235` gates `runSideEffect` on the permission **ceiling** only (`decideToolPermission`, `src/agent/PermissionLadder.ts:24-32`, a 32-line file that takes no consent argument and imports none), while `src/agent/StandingOrders.ts:410` **does** consult consent and denies at submit (`:113`) and every tick (`:143`) — and it does so **as a wrapper around the very same tool surface** (`:232 this.surface.tools.pan_at(...)`), so an LLM calling `tools.pan_at()` directly reaches identical game code with the guard skipped. `prospectorCan` (`src/game/Game.ts:5874`) still types only `'auto_collect' | 'auto_repair' | 'light_duty'`, so `auto_pan` remains structurally unaskable on the game loop. No commit after the s1313 filing touches the consent path. ⚠️ **Correction against F-1313-2's own text:** its clause *"at rung 2+ … unchecking it changes nothing"* is **narrowly FALSE** — unchecking `auto_pan` does kill LLM-issued HARVEST orders via `StandingOrders.ts:419`. It is true only in a plain boot with no agent submitting orders, which is the frame it was measured in. **This sharpens the asymmetry rather than weakening it:** `auto_pan` consent is honoured on the orders path, ignored on the direct-tool path, and unreadable on the game-loop path — **three entry points, three different answers.** ⓘ Trap for the next reader: `auto_pan` is **two unrelated things** — the consent ability, and an E2 Steamworks upgrade of the same id (`src/game/Upgrades.ts:100`) that is only a pan-tick multiplier while channeling. A grep hands you five progression-side hits that are not consumers. ➡️ **No new fork. Recommend the desk carry F-1313-2 and F-1313-3 as ONE line with one word owed: (a) or (b); s1313's recommendation was (b).**
