tasks/BACKLOG.md:5243:- ✅ **f1436-1 SHIPPED `1e3e67e1` (s1447) — F-1436-2 CURED: THE RED INVENTORY IS FINDABLE BY THE KEY PEOPLE ACTUALLY USE.** Review `reviews/f1436-1-red-inventory-lookup.md`. `scripts/red-inventory-lookup.mjs` (+171) answers *"is this red already known, and what sits behind it?"* per spec file or per exact test title, returning the recorded coordinate, blast-radius percentages, affected projects and error excerpt — the lookup F-1436-2 asked for after measuring that grepping ten cited `file:line` coordinates against the inventory returned **0 of 10** while grepping the same ten by TITLE returned **10 of 10**. ⭐ **THE DESIGN DECISION THAT MATTERS IS THE FAIL-SAFE OUTCOME SPLIT.** Three outcomes (`:141`) — `KNOWN-RED`, `CLEAN-IN-INVENTORY`, `NOT-IN-INVENTORY` — and a spec the inventory never covered returns **`NOT-IN-INVENTORY`, never "clean"**. That is the point rather than a detail: **F-1444-2's hazard is inventory membership being read as exoneration**, and a tool that answered "clean" for an unrecorded spec would have industrialised precisely that mistake. Exit codes measured at drain, all four branches, not inherited: known-red default **rc=0** · known-red `--strict` **rc=1** · not-in-inventory **rc=1** · nonexistent file **rc=1**. **EXECUTED on the merged tree, not merely built** (clean-tsc-is-not-evidence): inventory parse re-derived as **438 rows (303 failure, 135 blast-radius), 2388 tests run, 303 failed** — identical to the run log's claim, so confirmed rather than trusted. Gates in detached worktree `gate-s1447` (§3.0b): tsc clean · build green **1.18 s**, asset-diet unchanged at 54 plate-class PNGs · **`test:node-guards` rc=0** for the full chain (ticker-stats, findings-state, blocker-panel 76 rows/0 closed-on-panel, ruling-propagation 3 ruled/27 refusals/**0 stale**, desk-declaration 145/145) · new leaf **8/8** in 351 ms. **Adjacent suites: structurally none** — no `src/`, no `e2e/`, no contract data; the one gate surface it modifies IS `test:node-guards`, run in full above including `gate-caller-audit`, the guard that asserts a new npm-rooted script is genuinely rooted. No screenshots: nothing renders. Base `f0151de9`; main never moved any of the three files, so both scripts are **PURE ADD** and `package.json` is **LANE-TOUCHED only** (one entry inserted into the roster, alphabetical); all three landed blobs verified **byte-identical** to the lane tip. ⚠️ **A two-dot `main..lane/perf` diff advertised six extra files including a 142-line "deletion" of `tasks/art-drill-yard-stations-keyed.md` and an 8-line cut to `goals.json` — stale-base phantoms, not deletes**, because the lane branched at `f0151de9` before this same fire committed those files at `9d89770b`. Recorded because the phantom set named files the fire had just written, which is the most convincing possible form of that illusion. 🟢 **F-1447-2** (non-blocking, style): `:171` elides the parens on `A || (B && C)` — precedence makes it correct and the rc table proves all four branches, but a fail-safe expression deserves brackets the next time the file is opened; **no behaviour change requested.** ⓘ The tool reports the inventory, it does not audit it — so **F-1445-3 stays open**: `gt-02-slope.spec.ts:169` is recorded MOBILE-ONLY and was measured reddening on DESKTOP at s1445, and this lookup will faithfully repeat the stale qualifier until someone widens the row.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5246:- 🚀 **s1450 REFILL — lane-d DISPATCHED `f1440-2-perf-gate-without-a-frozen-baseline` after THREE fires of the debt being named and not paid.** s1447/s1448/s1449 each recorded a lane-d refill as owed; s1449 wrote *"this is now a pattern, not a coincidence, and the next fire should either refill lane-d or say why in the same words."* Paid. Leaf `f1440-2-perf-gate-baseline` flipped `planned → queued`. ✅ **EVERY PREMISE THE MASTER CITES WAS RE-VERIFIED ON CURRENT MAIN BEFORE DISPATCH, NOT INHERITED FROM THE AUTHORING FIRE (§2E stale-check, master is s1440-authored):** `452eb747` still resolves and still carries the withheld spec (12,620 bytes); `archive/lane-perf-s1440-452eb747` points at the same commit; `reviews/lane-e1-perf-pass.md` (10,865 bytes) and all four `artifacts/e1-perf-pass/census-{before,after}-*.json` baselines are on main; and `e2e/e1-perf-pass.spec.ts` is **genuinely absent from main** (`git cat-file -e` fatals), so this is a re-land and not an edit-in-place. **Never previously dispatched** — zero matches in `tasks/done/` and `tasks/failed/`, so this is a first attempt, not a retry. Dispatched in the **F-1424-3 order**: master already committed and tracked-clean → lane refreshed **second** (lane/perf was **32 behind**, reset --hard to `8f3b0474`, ahead=0 dirt=0) → premises re-proved **inside the refreshed lane** (all four present, and the spec correctly ABSENT there) → `cp` **third**. ⚠️ **A method note against my own convenience: the first verification pass was WRONG and is recorded so the shape is not repeated.** `ls tasks/done | grep -c f1440-2` printed `0` and **exited 1**, short-circuiting the `&&` chain into its `|| echo "CONFIRMED ABSENT"` — so a line reading CONFIRMED ABSENT was printed by the **failure branch**, having measured nothing at all, while the leaf and spec probes behind it never ran. A chained `grep` exit code can impersonate a measurement; the checks were re-run unchained before anything was queued. **GATE: F-1440-2 is retired when the re-landed spec is green in its DEFAULT path on a host that did not record the baseline, and the opt-in path is proved by a MANUFACTURED regression (rc=1), not by a green.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5247:- ✅ **s1451 — `f1440-2` SHIPPED `e21fa3d38760403d0161ea47deeb300554837429`: THE E1 PERF GATE IS BACK IN THE SHARED BATTERY, AND MACHINE-INDEPENDENT BY CODE PATH RATHER THAN BY LUCK.** F-1440-2 is **RETIRED** and its GATE (line above) is met on both halves. The cure is **five lines** against the withheld `452eb747` original: one `COMPARE_BASELINE` const, a three-line why-comment, and the same conjunct added to three existing `STAGE !== 'before'` guards. All **three** baseline `readFile` sites (`:141`, `:149`, `:242`) sit inside it, so with the flag off the default path *structurally cannot* read a committed baseline. ⭐ **THE GATE'S OWN GATE WAS THE CONTROL, NOT THE GREEN — because a green default path is consistent with two different worlds (the flag works / the comparison would have passed here anyway) and a passing test never executes its failing branch.** One variable flipped on the SAME tree, host, shell and `--workers=1`, both arms launched through one committed script (`artifacts/f1440-2/gate-battery.mjs`) so a flag could not drift between them: **default RC=0, 2/2 passed · opt-in RC=1, 2/2 failed on `e1-dry-gulch draw calls`, received 123, expected `<= 122`.** That reproduces the **REAL** F-1440-2 defect rather than the runner's synthetic one, proving the defect is **quarantined, not accidentally cured**. ⓘ **The runner's honestly-flagged owed different-machine run is no longer load-bearing:** the fire shell is a materially slower regime (F-1269-1) and is **exactly where s1440 measured the original spec RED**, so green-default/red-opt-in in the same minute settles it by code path, not hardware sample. Gates: tsc clean · build **957 ms** · node guards **RC=0** · ledger guards **RC=0** · **no adjacent reader** of `artifacts/e1-perf-pass` (by grep, independently re-derived). Pure **LANE-TOUCHED**, 8200 insertions **0 deletions**, `ort` clean, base `8f3b0474`; gated in a **detached worktree** on scratch port **5241** (§3.0b) so undecided content never entered main's tree. 🔺 **F-1451-1 (NON-BLOCKING, corrective `f1451-1-perf-census-publishes-over-retained-evidence` queued to lane-d): an ordinary battery run OVERWRITES retained tracked evidence.** `ARTIFACT_DIR` defaults to `artifacts/e1-perf-pass` and `STAGE` to `'after'`, and the spec is (correctly) not in `claimedByAnotherConfig`, so it now publishes over the committed `after/` arm. **Measured, not inferred: one project alone left 13 modified tracked files; both leave ~26.** The cost is not tidiness — any fire that runs a full battery on main then finds main **dirty**, contaminating the next drain's clean-main check, and it is precisely the s1294 / F-1295-1 hazard of 26 modified binaries awaiting someone's broad `git add`. **Not a RETENTION LAW violation** (tracked files; git keeps every version) and **not the runner's error** — the master ordered the census publication kept *"exactly as written"*, so curing it in-slice would have been a firewall violation; the runner flagged it and correctly declined. 🟡 **F-1451-2 — A CORRECTION TO THE RUNNER'S OWN SCOPE-5 ANSWER, CAUSED BY THE INSTRUMENT: the drift is WIDER than reported.** Desktop `e1-dry-gulch` is **137 vs a frozen 136**, reproducibly (runner 137/137/137, my census agrees) — but the opt-in loop asserts contract-by-contract and **stops at the first failure**, so everything after `e1-dry-gulch` was masked. Reading the full census instead of the failure shows **`e1-twin-banks` is ALSO +1 on BOTH projects** (desktop 127 vs 126, mobile 120 vs 119). Two contracts, both projects, all +1 — and both are exactly the maps that received terrain/water work since the baseline was recorded (`f1441-2` crossings, gt-05 water depth, tb-water-look; `65e3aaec` landed hours earlier). **That is legitimate feature work moving a frozen number, which is the strongest argument FOR this slice: an equality gate against a frozen draw count would have gone red for every one of those merges.** All p95 sit far under the 33.40 ms shed line (worst 19.9 ms) and moved in **both** directions vs baseline (desktop `the-claim` 15.5 vs 18.5 faster; `e1-dry-gulch` 18.1 vs 15.7 slower) — the wall-clock noise F-1440-2 named. Review `reviews/f1440-2-perf-gate-without-a-frozen-baseline.md`; instruments committed (`gate-battery.mjs` + both gate censuses), so every figure is re-derivable.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5248:- ✅ **f1451-1 SHIPPED `caae255148b4691f6ab77adb1cc4538bdfdaf1f1` (s1452) — F-1451-1 RETIRED: THE E1 PERF CENSUS PUBLISHES TO `latest/` AND NEVER OVER RETAINED EVIDENCE.** Review `reviews/f1451-1-perf-census-retained-evidence.md`. The cure is **three lines plus two `.gitignore` rules**: `STAGE`'s default moves `'after' → `'latest'`, so an ordinary `npx playwright test` writes `artifacts/e1-perf-pass/latest/` and `census-latest-*.json` — both ignored — and touches neither `before/` nor `after/`. Explicit `E1_PERF_STAGE=after` still works for a deliberate re-record. ⭐ **THE LOAD-BEARING PROOF IS DIRT, NOT THE GREEN, AND THE DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE FINDING: THIS SPEC ALWAYS PASSED — WHAT IT DID WRONG WAS *WHERE IT WROTE*.** Pass/fail therefore cannot discriminate between a working cure and no cure at all, so both arms ran through **one committed code path** (`artifacts/f1451-1/gate-battery.mjs`), same tree, host, shell, scratch server and `--workers=1`, differing in exactly one variable: **cured arm (no override, exactly as the shared battery runs it) RC=0 with `0` tracked files dirtied · control arm (`E1_PERF_STAGE=after`, the pre-cure default) RC=0 with `22` dirtied** (20 PNGs + both censuses). **Both arms GREEN** — which is precisely why the defect survived its own merge gate at `e21fa3d3` and is the reusable half of this row. ⚠️ **The zero was not believed until its subject was verified present** (a probe with no subject proves nothing): the cured arm wrote **20 PNGs + 2 census files** into `latest/`, and `git check-ignore -v` attributes them to `.gitignore:11` and `:12` by rule, not by accident. The control ran **last and inside the throwaway detached worktree** (§3.0b), so the 22 overwrites it deliberately caused never came near main; the worktree was restored to empty dirt and then removed. Gates: tsc clean · build **985 ms** · **`test:node-guards` RC=0** on the merged tree · slice spec **2 passed** desktop+mobile at `--workers=1`, and its own zero-console/page-error assertions on both projects put the boot probe *inside* the gate rather than beside it · gated on scratch port **5243**. Pure **LANE-TOUCHED**, base `7b918e3e`, main moved **none** of the three paths (its 3 intervening commits are all STATUS/task bookkeeping), `ort` clean, no graft. **Adjacent readers re-derived by grep rather than inherited:** the only other `E1_PERF_STAGE` reference in executable code is `artifacts/f1440-2/gate-battery.mjs:34`, which sets it **explicitly** to `'gate'` and so cannot be reached by a default change; nothing else reads `artifacts/e1-perf-pass`. 🟢 **F-1452-1 (non-blocking, no corrective owed): the blast radius is `22`, not the `~26` this ledger and the master both estimated** — 10 PNGs per project (5 maps × {plain, pressure}) + 1 census per project. The cure is sized by behaviour, not by the estimate, so nothing changes; it is recorded only so no later reader re-derives a 26 that was never true. ⚖️ **Gitignoring `latest/` is LAWFUL under the RETENTION LAW, ruled explicitly because pairing 'retention' with 'gitignore' in one slice deserves a stated ruling and not a silent one:** the law protects factory *history* from untracked deletion, while `latest/` is per-run regenerable churn cited by nothing, whose entire purpose is to stop the retained arms being overwritten — the slice **increases** retention safety, and deliberate evidence runs still publish under an explicit stage and stay tracked.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5251:- ✅ **F-1518-2 (s1518) — `e1-baron-fort-solidity` FLIPPED `stopped` → `superseded`: A STALE LEAD THAT HAD ALREADY COST ONE FIRE'S ATTENTION, AND WOULD HAVE COST THE NEXT ONE A DUPLICATE MASTER.** s1517's handoff named this leaf as the board's one live authoring lead — *"its premise may have flipped ... but check whether the leaf is superseded before re-authoring"*. Measured this fire: **superseded, not flipped**, by `f1452-1` (`4ab48743`, merged attended 2026-08-05). ⚠️ **WHAT MADE IT LOOK LIVE IS A NOTE THAT WAS TRUE WHEN WRITTEN AND FALSE FOR EVERY READER SINCE.** The s1452 dispatch row states in bold that `e1-baron-fort-solidity` *"stays **`stopped`**"* and that the successor *"supersedes nothing"* — written at **dispatch** time, before `f1452-1` ran, let alone merged. Nothing updated it when the successor landed three days later, so a leaf whose work is fully shipped kept advertising itself as one of the board's 5 stopped leaves, with an explicit-looking instruction not to treat it as superseded. **A dispatch-time claim about a future merge has an expiry date, and this one had no mechanism to notice it passed.** ✅ **EVIDENCE, ALL RE-MEASURED ON TODAY'S MAIN RATHER THAN READ OUT OF THE SUCCESSOR'S REVIEW:** **(1)** all four registry gaps the STOP's five-map census named are **PRESENT** in `assets/pilots/map-rebuild-spike/landmark-collision-contract.json` — `active_headframe` (the-claim), `fortified_far_bank`, `seized_headframe`, `siege_line` (e1-baron); **(2)** `e2e/fort-landmark-collision.spec.ts` asserts the four solids block **every face** *and* **release embedded heroes**, at the exact body counts 7 / 5 / 1 / 1; **(3)** `e2e/fort-static-routing.spec.ts` asserts `longestStationary < 120` fixed ticks — against the **>1,400** this leaf's own STOP measured — plus arrival at target, which is exactly the never-trap clause the STOP refused to trade away; **(4)** both specs **RUN GREEN on main today**: desktop-chrome, `--workers=1`, rc=0, **2 passed in 46.1s**. The root cause this leaf named (`BuildSystem.palisadeRoute()` iterating player-built palisades only, so static landmark blockers never enter the route graph) is precisely the seam the successor edited: **+139/−31 in `src/systems/BuildSystem.ts`**. ⚖️ **TWO THINGS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE, because superseding a leaf is narrower than it feels.** **(a)** `tasks/lane-baron-fort-solidity.md` **remains not-to-be-re-queued-unchanged** — the STOP was correct then and is correct now; retiring the leaf does not rehabilitate the master. **(b) F-BW-19 is NOT closed by this fire.** Its four census gaps are discharged and re-verified, but the finding was born from an **owner gate walk** (*"I was also able to walk through its fortress walls"*), and an owner-walk finding closes on an owner re-walk, not on a fire's green — the same discipline that kept F-1510-3 open this same fire on a cure that shipped. **REC to the owner: on the next Baron gate walk, try to walk the fortress walls; if they hold, F-BW-19 closes.** 📉 **BOARD EFFECT: the goal tree's stopped count drops 5 → 4, and the one lead two consecutive handoffs pointed authoring at is now correctly marked done-by-another-slice.** Related: [F-1439-4], [F-1439-5], [F-1452-1].
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5252:- ✅ **F-1519-1 (s1519) — `tb-stall-census` FLIPPED `stopped` → `superseded`: THE SECOND GHOST LEAF IN TWO FIRES, AND THIS ONE NAMED ITS OWN SUCCESSOR THREE DAYS BEFORE THAT SUCCESSOR MERGED.** s1518 ended by telling the next fire to check each remaining stopped leaf for a successor that had already shipped **before** re-authoring, on the grounds that one of five had turned out to be a ghost. Applied to the four survivors this fire: **one more was**. `tb-stall-census` (F-BW-14, the owner's *"the opponents have a lot of trouble finding their way"*) carries a DRAINED-PARTIAL stop whose last line reads **`Successor: f1441-2-crossings-keep-their-z.md`** — and that successor merged at **`65e3aaec`** on **2026-08-04**, together with `f1448-1`, while the leaf sat `stopped` for three days. ⚠️ **THE TELL IS DIFFERENT FROM s1518'S AND WORTH NAMING SEPARATELY.** F-1518-2's ghost was a *dispatch-time* note asserting the successor superseded nothing — a claim that expired. This one is the opposite failure: the leaf **correctly** recorded its successor by filename, in the right field, and still nobody walked the pointer. **A named forward reference is only as good as the pass that follows it**, and there is no such pass in the loop. Both leaves were retired by the same two-minute act; neither had a mechanism that would ever have performed it. ✅ **EVIDENCE, ALL RE-MEASURED ON TODAY'S MAIN, NOT READ OUT OF THE SUCCESSOR'S REVIEW:** **(1)** the withheld spec `e2e/twin-banks-never-wedged.spec.ts` — which the STOP says was preserved ONLY at `archive/lane-e2-arsenal-s1441-9236e9ba` — is **on main**, added by `5c27a1b5c` (runner lane-c, f1441-2), i.e. it reached main by another hand entirely, and it runs **GREEN on both projects today**. **(2)** The withheld `src/entities/Enemy.ts` change landed **z-aware**: `resolverCrossingAt(x, z)` at `:1429` takes BOTH coordinates and gravel bars go through `Terrain.gravelBarContains(bar, x, z)` — precisely the *"resolverCrossingAtX(x) tests x ONLY ... gravel bars are collapsed to an x-band with their z discarded"* defect the drain refused to ship. **(3)** The deep-water law the STOP was filed on is green: `expect(track?.deepSamples).toBe(0)` at `e1-twin-banks.spec.ts:213` sits inside the `assertFordRoute` helper, called twice by the test at `:122` (*routes enemies through both west and east fords*) — traced by **reading**, since the assertion is not in the test body and a grep for the test name would never have found it. **(4)** 12 executions, `--workers=1`, detached `gate-s1519` worktree (§3.0b custody): **10 passed / 2 failed**. 📊 **THE INVENTORY COMPARISON IS THE PART THAT EARNS THE VERDICT, AND IT WAS TAKEN WITH THE LOOKUP RATHER THAN A GREP.** `node scripts/red-inventory-lookup.mjs e2e/e1-twin-banks.spec.ts` returns **KNOWN-RED with FIVE failing rows** at the **2026-07-28** snapshot — which predates the successor merge by a week. Today exactly **2** of those 5 still fail, and they are the *builds sluices and stockpiles on both banks against one gold pool* rows (both projects), an **economy** assertion untouched by enemy routing. The **3** that went green are the routing rows: *routes enemies through both west and east fords* on **both** projects, plus *seeded Twin Banks diagnostics are stable* on mobile. ⚖️ **STATED HONESTLY RATHER THAN OVERSOLD: the red→green is a TEMPORAL fact plus a read mechanism, not a controlled bisect** — I did not re-run the 2026-07-28 tree, so I cannot exclude some other commit in that week. The mechanism (z-aware crossings) is read from the code and matches the STOP's own diagnosis word for word, which is why the verdict is supersession rather than a suspicion. **DELIBERATELY NOT DONE:** `tasks/lane-tb-stall-census.md` stays **not-to-be-re-queued** — the STOP was correct and retiring a leaf does not rehabilitate its master — and the leaf keeps **no `mergeHash`**, which its own reason says is deliberate precisely so the master refuses a re-queue. **REC to the owner: next time you play Twin Banks, watch whether the bandits still struggle to find their way; if they cross cleanly, F-BW-14 closes on your word rather than on my green** — the same discipline that left F-BW-19 open for you at s1518. 📉 **BOARD EFFECT: stopped count 4 → 3, and two of the original five turned out to be already-shipped work.** Related: [F-1518-2], [F-1441-2], [F-1441-3], [F-1448-1].
