tasks/BACKLOG.md:5276:🟡 **F-1438-1 (s1438, CONTROL-MEASURED — THE PERF GATE THAT PRICES NIGHT RENDER COST IS RED ON CLEAN MAIN, AND IS NOT IN THE INVENTORY).** `e2e/night3d-perf.spec.ts:67` ("daylight matrix and Night Shift pressure stay within the painted 115% p95 gate") asserts `terrain3dP95Ms / paintedP95Ms ≤ 1.15`. Measured on **clean main** in the fire shell this fire: desktop **2.054**, mobile **PASS / 1.5617 / 1.3938**. It reds on both projects, load-dependently, with no slice applied. ⚠️ **The consequence is not that a drain gets a false red — it is that this gate will wave through the NEXT night-render regression exactly as readily as it flagged this one**, because its verdict no longer varies with the thing it measures. It is simultaneously **absent from `logs/suite-red-inventory.md`** (only the *other* night3d-perf test, `:135`, is listed), so every future night drain must re-derive this control from scratch — this fire spent ~10 minutes and 6 test runs doing exactly that. 💡 The reusable half: **a gate that is red regardless of input has stopped being a measurement and become a constant**, and a constant red is indistinguishable from a constant green in every way that matters to the next drain. 🚫 **Do NOT cure this by raising the 1.15 ratio or extending the timeout** — that discards the only instrument that prices night render cost, which is precisely what the next slice will need. The real question is whether the ratio is unstable because of the fire shell's CPU ceiling (F-1269-1) or because `paintedP95Ms` has drifted on main. **GATE: inventory the red first (cheap, unblocks every future night drain), then measure the two hypotheses before touching any threshold.**
