tasks/BACKLOG.md:5292:- ⚠️ **AND THE REUSABLE HALF IS THAT THE CLASSIFIER, NOT THE LOOKUP, IS THE HARD PART — MY OWN FIRST DRAFT WAS WRONG IN EXACTLY THE WAY THE LESSON WARNS (a fresh classifier's first hits are probably its own bug).** Matching `HOLD|HELD|NOT MERGED|WITHHELD|BLOCKED` anywhere in the verdict LINE produced six hits of which **four were the instrument, not the board**: `e2-rail-tough-only-bind` (*"MERGE — the firewall **held** byte-for-byte"*), `f1420-1` (*"MERGE — gates green, scope 4's STOP condition **held**"*), `eight-winds-e2-row-order-survey` (*"**ACCEPTED** — the wiring slice stays **blocked**"*) and `art-e7-town-icons` (*"QA PASS — reference-tier, extraction **WITHHELD**"*). Every one is a MERGE verdict whose prose happens to discuss holding — **which is what review prose is about.** A guard reddening on those trains fires to ignore it, which is worse than no guard. ➡️ **The guard therefore classifies on the DECISION TOKEN ONLY** — the span before the first em-dash, period or comma — and all four false positives are pinned as fixtures so the sentence form cannot creep back. ⓘ **Lookup needed three keys, also measured rather than assumed:** of 13 mappable refusals, **6 resolved by file stem, 4 by `taskFile`, 3 by the Slice header**. `gg-03` is the instructive one — **its leaf id names the BLOCKED slice while its `taskFile` names the SUCCESSOR that discharged it**, so a `taskFile`-only lookup would never have found the stale row it was built to find.
