tasks/BACKLOG.md:5364:- 🔺 **F-1475-1 (s1475, MEASURED) — THE FAIRGROUND SOCKET MASTER WAS JOINTLY UNSATISFIABLE, AND THE RUNNER STOPPED EXACTLY WHERE IT WAS TOLD TO.** OWNER DESIGN FORK; nothing merged, nothing lost. ⚡ **WHAT HAPPENED:** run `20260806-052014-lane-a`, **124,606 tokens, ZERO repo changes**, lane `lane/a` left clean at `c47867388` (`ahead=0`), no gates run because there was no implementation to gate. The done-move is renamed `stopped-nomode-wall-s1475-*` so no later fire reads it as drainable. 🚪 **THE WALL, RE-DERIVED FROM THE CODE RATHER THAN INHERITED FROM THE RUNNER REPORT — the `HeadlessContractSim` constructor has exactly TWO doors and `e3-fairground` fails BOTH.** Door 1 rejects a requested mode the contract does not declare (`if (boot.mode && !mode) throw`), and **`e3-fairground` declares no `modes` field at all** — measured across the whole E3 contract file: `e3-blackout-ridge` undefined, `e3-moth-season` undefined, `e3-canyon-works` **one `escort` mode**, `e3-fairground` undefined. Door 2 rejects unsupported contracts when no mode is present (`if (!SUPPORTED_CONTRACTS.has(this.contractId) && !mode) throw`), and **`e3-fairground` is the one E3 contract absent from that set** — `e3-blackout-ridge`, `e3-moth-season` and `e3-canyon-works` are all admitted, **which is precisely how all three predecessor era-sockets were provable at all.** ➡️ **So fairground was never "the cheapest socket in the class"; it was the only BLOCKED one, and the property that blocked it is the one thing the authoring fire never probed.** 🔍 **THE MASTER’S ACCEPTANCE PATH CITED A MECHANISM THAT DOES NOT EXIST.** It told the runner to prove the socket *"through the declared-`mode` path that bypasses the support gate"* and to **reuse** the census spec’s *"forced generic diagnostic"* run for fairground. There is no such run: the string `fairground` appears **exactly ONCE** in `e2e/er01-e3-census.spec.ts` — inside the `SOCKET_GAPS` record, cited by content per F-1310-1 — and that file constructs **no** `HeadlessContractSim` for it. The two `mode: escort` constructions in that spec belong to `e3-canyon-works`. **The master therefore demanded a headless proof while its own NO #3 forbade the only construction path an unadmitted contract has.** ✅ **THE RUNNER WAS RIGHT, AND THE MASTER PRE-AUTHORIZED THIS OUTCOME IN THE SAME SENTENCE:** *"If it turns out no such path can drive your assertions without admitting the contract, STOP and report it as a finding — that is a genuine design fork about what admission means, and it belongs to the owner, not to this run."* That is a well-written stop condition doing its job; **the cost of the defect was one run, not a corrupted merge**, and that is the whole value of writing stop conditions into masters. ⭐ **WHAT s1474 GOT RIGHT, RECORDED SO THE NEXT AUTHOR DOES NOT OVER-CORRECT:** its browser-chain trace verified sound (`FerrisWheel.damage()` drops `spinning` on first hit → `syncFerrisWheelPower()` mirrors it via `set-node-online` from the sim tick → the 24 W producer starves both 6 W pavilion lamps → coverage drops → `autoSecureWaveForRun()` goes `MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`), as did its measurements that `twist.powerGrid` and `twist.dayNightCycle` are **already** socketed and that fairground has **no baron**. The socket SCOPE — extracting a headlessly-constructible Fair Wheel core out of `src/entities/FerrisWheel.ts` — remains sound and re-usable the moment the proof path is ruled on. 💡 **THE REUSABLE CLASS, and it is a new one: the master checked exhaustively that the SUBJECT was ready and never checked that the INSTRUMENT could be CONSTRUCTED.** Every probe it wrote asked about the wheel, the twist keys, the tick budget, the baron, the safe-dupe trap — all about the contents of the harness. **None asked whether the harness door opens for this contract.** ➡️ **Authoring rule earned here: an era-socket master must prove the harness can be INSTANTIATED for its target before it specifies one line of what to do inside it** — one construction probe, or one grep of `SUPPORTED_CONTRACTS` for the contract id, would have caught this before 124,606 tokens were spent. 🔺 **THE OWNER QUESTION — one word, and it unblocks the last E3 era-socket:** is there a **diagnostics-only** construction path that is explicitly **NOT** an admission? **RECOMMENDED (c):** a boot flag (e.g. `diagnostic: true`) that bypasses the support gate for MEASUREMENT while leaving `SUPPORTED_CONTRACTS` — the playability CLAIM — untouched, so the census headline stays **AGENT-READY 3 of 4** and the contract stays rejected until the crowd half is ruled on. **REJECTED BY THIS FIRE, with reasons: (a)** admitting `e3-fairground`, because admission asserts an agent can PLAY a contract whose advertised crowd-flock escort objective still has no consumer anywhere (`twist.fairground.crowdFlocks` sits in `DECLARED_INERT_PATHS` deliberately); **(b)** authoring a `modes` entry into contract data, which is inventing vocabulary to fit a harness — **Mistake #14, reject-do-not-stretch.** ⛔ **DO NOT RE-QUEUE THE MASTER UNCHANGED** — an identical retry of a premise already proven false is forbidden (§7.5); leaf `e3-fairground-socket` is now `blocked` / `blockClass: owner-fork` and `drain-block-check` STOPs on it.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5366:- ✅ **F-1475-2 (s1475, AUTHORED — SHIPPED s1476 at `1c8b344a`) — CLOSING F-1473-1: PIN THE POST-DEATH CRAWLER 3D STATE ON THE LITE AND FAILED TIERS.** `tasks/lane-f1473-1-crawler-dispose-tier-pin.md` → lane-a (leaf `f1473-1-crawler-dispose-tier-pin`), **TEST-ONLY**, firewall is the single file `e2e/wire-crawler-3d.spec.ts`. It closes the GATE F-1473-1 wrote for itself, verbatim: *"closes when a spec kills the full component ladder on the lite tier and pins the intended post-death `crawler3dState`."* 🔑 **GROUND TRUTH RE-DERIVED FROM THE CODE, NOT INHERITED FROM THE FINDING:** the broadened guard is live in `src/systems/CrawlerBossSystem.ts` as the single line `if (components.size === 0 && this.crawler3dState !== 'disposed') this.disposeCrawler3d('disposed');`, and `private disposeCrawler3d(nextState)` assigns `this.crawler3dState = nextState` before publishing to the canvas dataset — **so the expected post-death value is `disposed` on every tier.** The master states that expectation up front and rules that a differing measurement is a **FINDING**, not a licence to edit the assertion to match: *a test that pins whatever happened blesses the bug it was written to catch.* ⚠️ **THE F-1475-1 LESSON WAS APPLIED IN THE SAME FIRE THAT LEARNED IT — I PROBED WHETHER THE HARNESS COULD BE CONSTRUCTED BEFORE WRITING ONE LINE OF SCOPE, AND IT COULD NOT, AS-IS.** The existing kill ladder helper `destroyComponent()` calls `awaitMounted()`, which asserts `data-crawler3d-state` is `ready` on a 15 s timeout — but the lite tier publishes `lite` and the invalid-GLB tier publishes `failed`, **so a naive reuse fails 15 s per component on an assertion unrelated to the subject.** Everything else in that helper is tier-agnostic (suspend/restore HP edit, `crawlerParts`, `setBalance`, `launchBlastAt`, the gone-poll), so the mount-wait is a clean seam and scope item 1 is exactly that. **The master gives the trap away deliberately rather than letting the runner buy it** — which is the difference between this dispatch and the one that preceded it. ✅ **AUTHORING HYGIENE:** both probe keys measured **1 on main** at authoring time (F-1425-2), apostrophe-free and single-line — currency is a span of the F-1475-1 row above, safe-dupe is the count of disposed-state assertions in the spec, **which is 1 today and whose growth is the deliverable**, so the probe is semantic rather than incidental. Goal leaf registered in the same commit (Goal Registration Law); full F-1424-3 dispatch order followed (commit+push → refresh lane → re-prove keys in the lane → `cp`).
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5367:- 🟡 **F-1477-1 (s1477, measured while curing F-1476-1) — THE RENDERER-COUNT JSONs ARE ONLY 2 OF THE 12 TRACKED FILES THESE SPECS DIRTY. THE OTHER 10 ARE SCREENSHOTS, AND **0 OF 10 ARE BYTE-IDENTICAL ACROSS TWO CONSECUTIVE RUNS** — SO CLOSING F-1476-1 WILL NOT END THE CHURN IT CITES AS ITS MOTIVATION.** F-1476-1 justifies itself by the F-1407-1 artifact-churn class: *"any lane or fire running this spec is left with 12 dirty tracked files, which can trip a pre-flight's clean-vs-main test."* ✓ **The 12 is confirmed** (measured s1477 in a detached worktree after running both specs at `--workers=1`: 12 modified tracked files). ⚠️ **But the composition was never broken down, and it decides whether the cure works.** For the crawler+railcar pair the 12 are **10 PNGs + 2 JSONs**. `artifacts/s1477-noise/png-churn.mjs` ran both specs twice back-to-back on an unchanged tree and SHA-compared every screenshot: **0/10 identical** — all ten changed, every file, every run (e.g. `desktop-chrome-intact.png` `0891ce693800` → `33d733fdc690`). ➡️ **THEREFORE THE F-1476-1 CURE, WHICH IS CORRECTLY SCOPED TO THE JSONs, FIXES 2/12 AND A PRE-FLIGHT WILL STILL TRIP ON THE REMAINING 10.** This is the guard-denominator-narrower-than-the-cure shape: the finding's stated motivation is wider than the subject its gate names, so satisfying the gate honestly can still leave the motivating problem in place — and a reader who sees F-1476-1 go ✅ would reasonably conclude the churn is gone. 🧭 **The two halves genuinely want DIFFERENT cures, which is why they are two findings and not one task.** The JSON half is curable by recording what is reproducible (exact deltas + measured tolerance bands — F-1476-1, queued s1477), because the numbers carry real signal. Screenshots are **irreducibly non-deterministic** at byte level, so the honest options for the PNG half are: stop tracking them and retain them as untracked evidence under the RETENTION LAW's mirroring rule; or keep them tracked and stop treating a dirty `artifacts/**` as a pre-flight failure; or compare them perceptually rather than by byte. **All three are policy choices with a cost, so this is a RULING, not a mechanical fix.** **GATE: closes when a ruling picks one of the three for tracked screenshot evidence AND the pre-flight clean-vs-main test is reconciled with it.** ⓘ Scope note, stated so nobody over-reads this: measured for `wire-crawler-3d` + `wire-railcar-3d`, `desktop-chrome`, this machine. Other specs that write screenshots are **unmeasured** and are likely the same class.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5369:- ✅ **F-1476-1 (s1476, drain of `f1473-1-crawler-dispose-tier-pin`) — THE RETAINED RENDERER-COUNT "BASELINES" ARE NOT A REPRODUCIBLE PROPERTY OF A COMMIT. THEY ARE PARTLY RUN-TO-RUN NOISE, AND THE SPEC REWRITES 12 TRACKED FILES EVERY RUN.** NON-BLOCKING for the merged slice, proved by a control arm. ⚠️ **THIS ROW WAS REWRITTEN LATER IN THE SAME FIRE THAT FILED IT — the first version asserted the baselines were "stale on main" and prescribed refreshing them "in a commit that names the main-side change responsible". I then MEASURED that instruction and it is partly unsatisfiable: for the `calls`/`triangles` component there IS no responsible change to name.** 🔬 **HOW IT WAS MEASURED, since the first framing looked airtight:** running `e2e/wire-crawler-3d.spec.ts` dirties 10 PNGs plus both `artifacts/wire-crawler-3d/renderer-counts-*.json`, with deltas across all five recorded phases (desktop `coldBaseline` `calls 72→74`, `triangles 146026→147708`, `geometries 80→81`). A three-way comparison first cleared the slice: control on **pure main** vs committed = DIFFERENT; **merged arm vs control arm = BYTE-IDENTICAL**; so the slice causes zero renderer change and the drift belongs to main. **Then the attempt to attribute that drift is where the real finding lives.** A bisect harness was built and PROVED CONSTRUCTIBLE first (the F-1475-1 lesson applied): a detached worktree with **the instrument PINNED and the subject VARIED** — today's `e2e/wire-crawler-3d.spec.ts` + `playwright.config.ts` + `scripts/external-server-guard.mjs` checked out over each candidate commit, **14.4 s per step**. ⓘ *The overlay needs that third file because today's config requires the F-1457-1 guard that only landed 08-05; without it every old commit dies `MODULE_NOT_FOUND` — a harness that would have failed identically at all 1,092 commits and converged on an innocent one.* 📉 **The predicate then failed its own validation, BOTH ways:** committed `72` (07-25) → `73` (07-31) → **`72`** (08-02) → `74` (main) is **non-monotonic**, so bisect's precondition is violated outright. The discriminating control — **the same commit `a1ec923b8`, same shell, back-to-back** — returned `calls 72 / triangles 146026` and then `calls 73 / triangles 146028`. ➡️ **The ±1 `calls` / ±2 `triangles` component is NOISE, not drift, and no commit can be blamed for it.** ⚖️ **What survives as possibly real:** `geometries 80→81` held steady at 80 across both runs at `a1ec923b8` and reads 81 on main, as does the large `+1682` triangle step — those are **unattributed and NOT bisectable by this predicate**; attributing them needs a denoised statistic (n runs per commit, compare distributions) rather than a byte comparison. 💡 **THE REUSABLE LESSON, which is bigger than this artifact: a number committed as "retained evidence" implies a reproducibility it was never measured to have.** Every reader since 07-25 — including this fire's own first draft, and s1475's standing order to confirm the baselines "byte-unchanged" — treated byte-equality as the pass condition for a quantity that is not byte-stable. A fire obeying that order literally would have blocked a clean slice on someone else's noise. **GATE: closes when either (a) the counts are re-characterised with a measured noise band and the artifact records a tolerance instead of a byte, or (b) the spec stops writing them into the tracked tree.** Until then the churn is the **F-1407-1 artifact-churn class** — any lane or fire running this spec is left with 12 dirty tracked files, which can trip a pre-flight's clean-vs-main test. **Do NOT "refresh" these baselines as a drive-by: it would bless one sample of a noisy quantity as truth and restart the same misreading.** 📐 **MEASURED s1477 — THE DENOISED STATISTIC THE GATE ASKED FOR NOW EXISTS, AND IT CORRECTS THIS ROW ON ONE POINT OF RECORD.** `artifacts/s1477-noise/REPORT.md` (`1b64d48b1`): **crawler n=12, railcar n=10, one commit `659f4efe0`, instrument pinned, `--workers=1`, same shell** — subject fixed, so any spread IS the band. **(1)** Most metrics are already byte-stable: **18 of 20** (crawler), **10 of 18** (railcar). **(2)** `geometries`/`textures` jitter is **COMMON-MODE — every phase-to-phase delta is EXACT** (all 6 railcar phase pairs, single value across all 10 runs: `geometries` 8, 5, 5, −3, −3, 0). The absolutes move; the differences do not, which is why the railcar spec’s live `geometries - baseline.geometries < 8` assertion is sound and has never flaked. **(3)** `calls`/`triangles` jitter **independently per phase**, with a `+1 call ⟺ +2 triangles` signature in **every** sample of **both** specs — the fingerprint of a single 2-triangle quad present or absent in the sampled frame, i.e. a sampling race rather than a rendering change. ⚠️ **THE CORRECTION: this row recorded `geometries 80→81` as "stable across both runs… unattributed and NOT bisectable". At n=12 `coldBaseline.geometries` is HARD-STABLE at 81 (12/12), so it was never the noisy component — it is a reproducible property of the commit and IS attributable.** ⚠️ **AND THE TRAP THE MEASUREMENT EXPOSED: a metric cannot be classified by its NAME.** The same `geometries` is hard-stable in the crawler’s `coldBaseline` and noisy in **four** railcar phases — noise is a property of the **(spec, phase, metric) triple, measured**, so any global whitelist is wrong on this evidence. ✓ **VERIFIED s1477 and load-bearing for the choice of cure: NOTHING READS THESE FILES** — `git grep renderer-count` over all tracked files returns the two specs that WRITE them and otherwise only prose (reviews, BACKLOG, STATUS, masters). No consumer, no guard, no assertion against the committed JSON. ➡️ **CURE QUEUED s1477 as gate disjunct (a), not (b): `tasks/lane-f1476-1-renderer-count-tolerance.md` → lane-a**, goal leaf `f1476-1-renderer-count-tolerance`. **(a) beats (b) because the deltas are exact and carry the regression signal, so "stop writing them" would discard real evidence to fix churn** — the task records exact values + exact deltas, bands with provenance for the jittery triples, and makes an out-of-band value FAIL loudly, converting a write-only churn source into an actual guard. **`mobile-chrome` remains UNSAMPLED — the master orders the runner to measure it (n≥8) rather than copy the desktop bands.** The closing prohibition below still stands and is restated in the master: do NOT refresh the old-format baselines as a drive-by. ✅ **CURED AND MERGED s1478 at `3ab612ed647f8e2fe7a697faa2213ba9cdbf433e`** (review `reviews/f1476-1-renderer-count-tolerance.md`, lane tip `7b934b643`, base `14028bd8e`). Gate disjunct **(a) tolerance-not-byte** shipped: `e2e/renderer-count-artifact.ts` turns the four artifacts from write-only evidence into **live expectations** — exact values where s1477 measured hard stability (`coldBaseline.geometries: 81`, 12/12), `{measured, tolerance}` bands with one-quantum headroom where it jitters, and **exact** phase-to-phase `geometries`/`textures` deltas where the jitter is common-mode. Every band carries a `provenance` block (`n`, `commit`, `project`, `headroom`) so it is re-derivable, and the runner measured `mobile-chrome` itself rather than copying desktop bands (`artifacts/f1476-1/samples-*-mobile-chrome.json`). 📊 **Gates, all re-run on the merged tree rather than inherited:** tsc clean · build ✓ 2.28s · slice specs **14 passed** both projects at `--workers=1` · `test:node-guards` **rc=0** (run because a new `e2e/` module is a collection surface) · zero console errors (asserted in-spec). ✅ **BOTH ACCEPTANCE TESTS REPRODUCED, NOT TAKEN ON TRUST — and the second is the load-bearing one:** (1) after a full gate run `git status -- artifacts/` shows **0** `renderer-counts-*.json` entries, down from all four every run; (2) a **manufactured** out-of-band band `[0, 1]` made the spec fail with the band branch’s own message — `renderer count coldBaseline.calls=74 outside band [0, 1]`, **1 failed** — then restored byte-identical. A passing guard never executes its violation path, so its green says nothing about whether it can fail (s1299/s1300 standard). ⓘ Merge was **zero-conflict**: all 12 lane paths LANE-TOUCHED only against the 16 main moved since base. **No `src/` bytes**, so the F-1460-1 sim-pin trigger does not fire, DEPLOY correctly skips, and **no GZ-01 gazette item is owed** — the filter law wants a player-visible change, and this one is invisible in play.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5380:🔺 **F-1458-1 (s1458, MEASURED — A ZERO-CONSOLE ASSERTION THAT CAPTURES NODE OWN WARNING HANDLER REDS 8/8 ON THE PINNED INTERPRETER, AND THE OBVIOUS CURE DOES NOT WORK.)** `e2e/er01-e2-census.spec.ts` (test "… census support is explicit and deterministic"; the file is NOT on main — the drain refused it, so cite it by content, F-1310-1) hooks `console.error`/`console.warn` into a `consoleErrors` array and asserts `expect(consoleErrors).toEqual([])`. **Node default `warning` handler prints through `console.error`**, and `process.emitWarning` delivers on a LATER TICK, so a process-level warning triggered during module init lands inside the window where the spec has the console hooked. Node ≥26 emits exactly one on first read of `globalThis.localStorage` without `--localstorage-file`. **Measured, same tree, `--workers=1`: house command 8 FAILED/8 · `NODE_OPTIONS=--no-warnings` 8 PASSED (47.1s) · with the cure 8 PASSED (48.5s).** So every substantive assertion already held; only the channel was wrong. ⚠️ **THE OBVIOUS CURE WAS MEASURED AND FAILED: stubbing `globalThis.localStorage` first — the house `scripts/gr-sim.test.mjs` pattern, and the pattern this spec already uses for `location`/`window` — left it STILL 8/8 RED**, because the triggering read happens deep in the vite-SSR module graph before the test body runs. Recording the dead end matters as much as the cure: prescribing it unmeasured would have sent the corrective into it. ✅ Working cure (own the warning channel, restore in `finally`) at `artifacts/f1458-1/er01-console-capture-cure.patch`, blob `0867a696` → `06fd7284`. ⚖️ **Stated against my own cure:** suppressing all process warnings widens the silence, and the narrow alternative pins to one warning text — **the corrective must PICK and justify, and that unresolved trade is exactly why this was refused rather than fixed at drain time.** A gate should not be quietly widened by the unreviewed author of its own fix. ✅ **CLOSED s1458 — corrective `f1458-1` SHIPPED `f7632e33`, merged in `96d40988`. The runner chose the NARROW filter (option b): only the exact Node warning text is dropped, so a genuine app `console.error` still reds the spec — RE-PROVEN ON THE PINNED NODE by planting one (4/4 red). Its stated ceiling: a Node rewording reds the test until the filter is updated, which is the honest trade and is written into the spec. GATE: none.**
