tasks/BACKLOG.md:5448:- ✅ **F-ER02-1 CORRECTIVE ATTEMPT 2 — SHIPPED s1531 (merge `51b0bc35`, 594.2x). AUTHORED + DISPATCHED s1529 → `lane-b-f1529-1-order-failure-coalesce-by-identity.md` (lane-b, FIRE-AUTHORED), leaf registered `queued` in the same commit. CHANGED PREMISE per §7.5 — an identical retry is forbidden and this is not one.** ⭐ **THE UPPER-BOUND ARM IS WHY THIS IS STILL FIRE-AUTHORABLE, AND IT IS THE MOST VALUABLE NUMBER ON THIS ROW: deleting the `order_failure` surprise outright gives 2,971 calls → **4** (`fnv1a32:8c71d437`), 0.29 → 5.13 waves/s — a **743x** reduction.** So the reviews §3.1 mechanism is **CONFIRMED IN FULL**, s1528 picked the **right option** of the three, and **only the KEY was wrong.** That is a far smaller gap than a design fork, and it gives the next attempt a yardstick instead of an opinion. 🔓 **Coalescing is INFORMATION-PRESERVING, which is the argument that keeps this a repair rather than an owner fork — and it was measured, not assumed:** `now.orders` carries `{status:failed, reason:INVALID_TARGET: gold-seam-999 is unavailable.}` on **every single view**. The surprise is the *notification* channel; the view is the *state* channel — suppressing a duplicate notification hides nothing and only stops **billing a turn to repeat an answer the view already contains** (`outcome.calls` is what AP-07 exports as `num_turns`). 📐 **THE MASTER DELIBERATELY PRESCRIBES AN ACCEPTANCE, NOT A MECHANISM (F-1529-1) — the direct lesson of attempt 1.** The runner **chooses the key** and must justify it in its report; the only hard constraint is that it **must survive `submit()`** (the record and its `id` are measured dead). The gate is numeric: the after/before `calls` ratio against the 743x bound, **plus** a both-directions proof that a second, **DIFFERENT** failure is still surfaced — *a cure that silences it is worse than the storm.* A measured BLOCKED is explicitly named a legitimate outcome. 🚫 **`claim_damage` stays held out (F-1529-4):** no status/reason pair to de-dupe against, so it needs a rate-limit or a window — an **attended/owner pick**, on the desk. All four citation keys proved **1 on main** before being written down (F-1425-2), each on one visible line; the fourth is the s1529 review upper-bound hash, so a **0 accuses lane staleness rather than the file.** **GATE: drain when lane-b reports; before/after `outcome.calls` STATED WITH ITS RATIO against the 743x bound, the runners chosen KEY and its justification, both pins named individually (`byte-for-byte` and `E2 Baron`) with any move reported and NEVER re-pinned (F-1441-3), the both-directions evidence, `node -v` per F-1527-3, and `git diff --name-only` showing exactly `src/agent/StandingOrders.ts` + `scripts/gr-sim.test.mjs`.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5449:- ✅ **F-ER02-1 SHIPPED s1531 — THE SURPRISE STORM IS CURED AT 594.2x, AND THE HEADLINE WAS RE-DERIVED AT THE GATE RATHER THAN INHERITED (Mistake #4).** Merge `51b0bc35c6c02f6b35dbf4cff24929d3cb310941`; lane tip `04a92f855`; review `reviews/f1529-1-order-failure-coalesce-by-identity.md`. 📊 **BOTH ARMS RE-RUN BY THE DRAINING FIRE** from `logs/runs-archive/s1529-gate-driver-storm-arms.mjs`, `the-claim`/`e1-the-claim-01`, Node 26.4.0: **BEFORE (stock main) 2,971 calls / `fnv1a32:d93e9b89` / 0.32 waves/s → AFTER (merged) 5 calls / `fnv1a32:c3952a80` / 5.22 waves/s.** The BEFORE arm reproduced s1529 baseline to the digit, which is the only thing that makes the AFTER number mean anything. **594.2x = 80.0% of the 743x deletion-only ceiling — while KEEPING the surprise channel that deletion would have destroyed.** 🔑 **THE KEY, which is the whole design content: a SEMANTIC ORDER TUPLE mapped to its last failure reason, held in a `Map` BESIDE the records rather than inside them** — so it survives `submit()` rebuilding `this.records` from scratch, which is exactly what killed attempt 1. Success `delete`s the key; `reset()` clears the map. ✅ **BOTH DIRECTIONS PROVED, and scope 3 was the hard acceptance: a reordered-but-semantically-identical BUILD coalesces WITHOUT advancing the surprise sequence, while fail → success → fail is NEWS AGAIN at a strictly greater `seq` and `needsRider:true`.** ⓘ **The runner caught a flaw in its OWN first key** — raw `JSON.stringify(order)` is sensitive to nested property insertion order, so `{x,z}` vs `{z,x}` would have defeated the coalesce; it replaced it with a positional tuple and added the reordered-BUILD regression. 🧪 **GATES: tsc 0 errors · build green 2,182 modules · `test:node-guards` rc=0, 353 pass / 0 fail · BOTH PINS GREEN BY NAME (*gr-sim replays … byte-for-byte*, *the E2 Baron fights keep their pinned outcomes*) and NEITHER MOVED, so no re-pin was needed or made (F-1441-3) · `ap-standing-orders` 12/12 desktop+390px · `044-start-screen` 14/14.** 🟨 **The 6 reds in `061-first-claim-onboarding` are PRE-EXISTING and that is a MEASUREMENT, not a fingerprint argument: the 2026-07-28 inventory match was 10 days stale, so a CONTROL ARM was run on stock main and reproduced all six identically.** 🔒 Gated in a detached worktree (§3.0b custody); main took content only after the verdict. **Firewall held exactly two files, append-only (+100/−0); the four existing tests were not edited, so the F-1528-2 carve-out was never invoked.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5455:- 🔺 **F-1529-4 OPEN — OWNER PICK: `claim_damage`, the SECOND storm trigger, needs a RATE-LIMIT or a WINDOW.** Filed s1529 alongside the F-ER02-1 work, and held out of both `f1528-1` and `f1529-1` on purpose rather than forgotten. 📊 The measurement, from `reviews/standing-orders-rehearsal-e2.md`: `claim_damage` fires on **every** `building_damaged` event, and on `e2-incline` that multiplied protocol cost **7.75×** for **0** extra waves. 🔑 **WHY A FIRE MAY NOT AUTHOR IT, stated precisely because its sibling F-ER02-1 *was* fire-authorable:** the `order_failure` cure restores an invariant the file already asserts and is **information-preserving** (`now.orders` carries the failure on every view, so suppressing a duplicate notification hides nothing). **`claim_damage` has NO status/reason pair to de-dupe against** — there is no invariant to restore and no equivalent state channel, so any cure must *invent* a policy about how much damage news is enough. That is a design choice with rider-visible consequences, i.e. §7.3 owner territory. 💡 **RECOMMENDATION: a WINDOW (coalesce all claim damage within N seconds into one surprise) rather than a rate-limit.** A window degrades gracefully under a burst — the rider is told once per window and the *last* event still lands; a rate-limit silently drops the tail, which is the failure mode that would hide a claim actually falling. **GATE: retires when the owner picks window or rate-limit (and names N); the master is then fire-authorable in one lane run against the same `outcome.calls` yardstick F-1529-3 established.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5456:- ✅ **F-1528-3 OPEN — EVIDENCE FOR THE OWNER'S DESK, NOT A DEFECT: the headless PROSPECTOR already has a moving body, so F-1499-2 is narrower than it reads.** Filed s1528 while draining `f1526-1` (`fa9fbda1`), which published `now.prospector` on both doors. Measured immediately afterwards from the shipped CLI (`gr-sim --contract=e2-incline --seed=e2-incline-01 --policy=idle`), four consecutive turns: **prospector `-25.8/-19.07` → `-25.98/-19.42` → `-25.68/-19.08` → `-25.6/-19.21`, while the hero sat at `-24/-18` for the entire run.** ⭐ **Why it matters to the ruling:** F-1499-2 asks *"does a headless rider get a body?"* and gates the whole ER-03 wave (F-ER02-5/6 wait on it). Read literally, that question sounds like *does embodiment exist headlessly at all* — and **it does, and it moves.** What does **not** move is the **hero**, which is what makes the secure condition geometrically unreachable (F-ER02-5: both maps park the hero at exactly 12.0 wu from the rail its own wave-12 Railcar rides, against `sparkRig.range` 10, and it finished every trestle run at 100/100 HP, never touched). ➡️ **So the fork the owner is actually being asked to rule on is about the HERO/rider specifically, not about embodiment in general — and the Prospector's existing movement path is a worked precedent sitting in the same sim, which should make the "grow a movement path" arm cheaper than it looked when the question was written.** 🚫 **This does NOT answer F-1499-2 and must not be read as answering it** — a fire may not decide a design fork; it narrows the question and prices one arm. **GATE: no work is unblocked by this row; it retires when F-1499-2 is ruled.** (Recorded here with its own row rather than buried inside the `f1526-1` drain row, per the Completeness Law — an owner item mentioned only inside another finding's row is on no board the owner reads.) ✅ **CLOSED (owner 2026-08-09): folded into the ERA-VERBS PROGRAM greenlight (see F-MTS-2) — the evidence stands