ver the lane fleet alone.** ✅ **ONE CORRECTION TO MY OWN FINDING, MADE BEFORE IT COULD MISLEAD: the warning above must NOT be read as "the refresh cannot discriminate load."** The shipped s1688 run already solves that — its **scope-5 serial attribution pass** re-ran all 199 failing spec files serially (1,706 executions, ~5.1 h) and split **273 load-attributable** from **274 genuinely red**, i.e. *roughly half the apparent explosion was the machine.* ➡️ **So the owed act is not a new mechanism: it is to RE-RUN THE PROVEN PROCEDURE, SERIAL ARM INCLUDED, ON A QUIET BOX.** A refresh that drops that arm to save wall clock would be strictly worse than the stale snapshot, and F-2254-1 is the standing evidence for why. 📋 **STATE FOR THE NEXT FIRE, so nobody re-derives it: the old master `lane-a-f1643-2-suite-red-inventory-refresh.md` is `⛔ ALREADY SHIPPED — DO NOT QUEUE` (leaf `merged` @ `1981d2b3a86edc3d9872e3127ccb5369ab09cffc`; Mistake #8 — the instrument refuses it correctly, so a NEW master is owed, not a re-queue). Current snapshot `2026-08-11`, now 12 days old against a 7-day threshold, with 148 commits touching `e2e/` or `src/` since. Budget from the precedent: 6 h 32 m 52 s wall at `nice -n 19 --workers=3` under the owner's throttle law, and the runner's auto-commit WILL sweep the ~150 MiB `logs/suite-red-inventory-raw.json` into the lane tip, so plan the path-scoped drain in advance (the s1688 precedent quarantines it to an `archive/*` ref rather than `merge --no-ff`).**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:5506:- 🔎 **F-2259-1 `desk-birth-guard` CLAIMS "EVERY OWNER-GATED ROW FILED THIS WINDOW REACHED THE DESK" OVER A CORPUS ITS PARSER SILENTLY NARROWS (s2259, NON-BLOCKING, CURED SAME FIRE).** The PASS ranges over whatever `addedRows()` admitted, and that parser takes a line only if it **LEADS** with one of four glyphs after whitespace, or contains the literal `**F-`. A row contributing to neither cannot enter `qualifying`, so it is **structurally incapable of failing the gate** — F-2256-1's shape one file over, in the sibling guard, one fire later. 🔑 **WHY IT SURVIVED: THE TWO ADMISSION TESTS EACH COVER THE OTHER'S BLIND SPOT, so a row escapes ONLY when it uses BOTH house conventions AT ONCE** — a markdown bullet before the glyph **and** the bracketed `**[F-id]` key. Measured: `🔺 **F-9999-1**` admitted (glyph) · `- 🔺 **F-9999-1**` admitted (`**F-`) · `🔺 **[F-9999-1]` admitted (glyph) · `- 🔺 **[F-9999-1]` **ESCAPED**. Neither convention is exotic — the live BACKLOG carries **353** bullet-then-glyph rows, **47** bracketed keys and **38** lines in the escaping shape today; glyphs outside the admitted four (🔵, 👑, 🟢) escape outright. 📐 **THE FILE PRICED THE WRONG TWO THINGS, AND ITS OWN HEADER SHOWS IT:** the 2×2 table at the top prices the SELECTOR (which gates count) against MEMBERSHIP (what the desk carries), over 25 windows and 96 rows — and never prices the CORPUS both range over. 📊 **MEASURED over all 2,459 BACKLOG-touching commits: 10 owner-gated keyed rows escaped, of which 5 are REAL owner items** — `F-1637-2` and `F-1625-4` (**BOTH ON ROBIN'S DESK TODAY**), `F-1617-1`, `F-BT-2` (🔵) and `F-1118-2` (👑); the declaration is non-zero in **15** real handoff windows. ⚖️ **SEVERITY, HONESTLY AND NOT INFLATED: LATENT.** Replaying all **1,133** real handoff windows, every one of those rows had already been desked by hand in the same handoff, so the blindness has never actually cost a desk entry. What it costs is the guarantee: the PASS cannot mean what it says. 🚦 **CURED BY DECLARATION, AND THE RESTRAINT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN STYLISTIC — THE OBVIOUS CURE WAS TRIED AND REJECTED ON DATA.** Teaching the two tests the two house conventions was replayed over those same 1,133 windows: it admits **168** extra rows across **85** windows and produces exactly **THREE** new verdicts, **ALL THREE FALSE** (`F-1631-3` *"OFF THE OWNER'S DESK — CLOSED … OWES NO WORD"*, `F-1635-3` *"SUPERSEDED s1636"*, `325241e2` *"DRAINED s1497"*) — **catching ZERO genuine undesked rows.** And this guard's REMEDY is *"put it on the desk"*, so obeying a false red **manufactures owner-desk items out of closure records**, onto a desk already at 27 (s2255's rule: the harm is the remedy, not the red). Restricted to the QUALIFYING set, not the corpus (s2230) — naming all 168 excluded rows would be inflation dressed as diligence. ✅ **BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL BEFORE LANDING (F-1274-2): 8/8 channels across 4 fixtures × 2 modes, verdict + rc + stderr unchanged and every pre-cure stdout line preserved** — with the control asserting its own validity first (F-2215-1), which is what caught my first attempt: the pre-cure copy died `MODULE_NOT_FOUND` having never run, because I copied the one relative import I noticed and the file has **three**. 🔒 `scripts/desk-birth-row-denominator-guard.test.mjs`, 10 arms, rooted in `test:ledger-guards`; **each over-general cure is caught by exactly the reverse control built for it** — widening membership (the measured-bad cure) reds 1+6+7+10, reporting the whole corpus reds 4+5, refusing instead of declaring reds **7 ALONE**, declaring only on failure reds **8 ALONE**, a count without names reds **7 ALONE**, dropping the admitted-set test reds 2+3+8. ⓘ **REACHABILITY AUDIT (s2226's duty): arm 9 reddened in NO variant** — not a weak arm but one no variant I had built could reach, i.e. indistinguishable from decoration until tested; an eighth variant (downgrade the FAIL to advisory) reaches it, and **all 10 arms now redden behaviourally